\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,173 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2011\par 12:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EFSF TO HOLD OFF ON EUR3B BOND DUE TO MARKET VOLATILITY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Financial Stability Facility, the euro zone's temporary rescue fund, plans to hold off on selling a new EUR3 billion bond due to volatile market conditions, people familiar the situation said Wednesday. (published 1035 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GREEK CABINET APPROVES REFERENDUM PLAN, VOTE SEEN BY CHRISTMAS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece's cabinet Wednesday approved plans for a referendum on the country's latest bailout package, despite an open revolt by ruling-party lawmakers and amid fears the referendum could stoke further uncertainty in world financial markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OECD INFLATION RATE ROSE FOR THIRD STRAIGHT MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The annual rate of inflation across developed economies picked up for the third straight month in September as prices for goods and services other than food and energy rose at the fastest pace since April 2009. (published 1100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE MANUFACTURERS FALL DEEPER INTO CONTRACTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone manufacturing activity contracted in October at its steepest rate since July 2009, adding to signs that growth is grinding to a halt just a day before the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decides whether or not to cut its key interest rate. (published 1024 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN OCTOBER UNEMPLOYMENT RATE INCHES UP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Germany's rate of unemployment inched up slightly in October, the first increase in more than two years, but the overall number of jobless fell further below the closely watched three million mark. (published 1013 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALY CABINET TO MEET WED EVENING TO DISCUSS MEASURES -SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi has called an extraordinary cabinet meeting for Wednesday evening to discuss new economic measures that could be announced ahead of the meeting of leaders from the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations scheduled to start in Cannes, France, Thursday, people familiar with the situation said Wednesday. (published 1038 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN LABOR MINISTER: GERMANY DEPENDENT ON FUNCTIONING EURO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Labor Minister Ursula von der Leyen warned Wednesday that Germany's export economy is dependent on a strong euro, after the labor ministry announced a slight rise in unemployment for October. (published 1052 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KREMLIN AIDE: RUSSIA WOULD CONSIDER EXTRA SUPPORT FOR EU FUND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia's chief presidential economic aide Wednesday said Moscow, if asked, would consider contributing money directly to the European Union debt bailout, in addition to planned contributions via the International Monetary Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY'S SCHAUEBLE EXPECTS GREECE TO EXECUTE REFORMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said Greece's fellow euro-zone countries would lend their support should the country decide to implement the tough reforms agreed upon in the latest aid program, the German daily Financial Times Deutschland reported Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SNB DECLINES TO COMMENT ON RUMOR IT WAS IN THE MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Wednesday declined to comment on market rumors it was in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the Swiss franc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPEC OCT OIL OUTPUT DOWN ON SAUDI CUTS, IRAQ PROBLEMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil production from the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries fell in October as cuts in Saudi Arabia and problems in Iraq more than offset the recovery in Libya's following the fall of the Gadhafi regime. (published 1041 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IRISH PM: IRELAND WILL REPAY ITS DEBTS; WILL NOT FOLLOW GREECE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ireland cannot follow Greece into defaulting on its debts as this would be "disastrous" for the country's embryonic economic recovery, Irish Prime Minister Enda Kenny said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IRISH FIN MIN: ECB HAS NO CHOICE BUT TO BUY TROUBLED EURO-ZONE BONDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has no choice but to buy the bonds of troubled euro-zone nations on secondary markets after the renewed turmoil sparked by Greece's decision to hold a referendum, Irish Finance Minister Michael Noonan said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PREPARING EMERGENCY BOND SWAP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is preparing an emergency maneuver to swap state bonds held by the country's banks for new bonds with a longer maturity, according to Italian newspaper Il Messaggero Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S SWAN URGES GREECE TO STICK WITH BAIL-OUT PLAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's Treasurer Wayne Swan Wednesday criticised the Greek government's decision to hold a referendum on the nation's EUR130 billion bail-out plan and urged authorities to instead quickly implement the agreement struck by European leaders in Brussels last month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ SHIRAI CONCERNED OVER IMPACT OF EURO CRISIS ON JAPAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan policy board member Sayuri Shirai said Wednesday that one of the most serious downside risks to the Japanese economy is the negative effects of the European sovereign debt crisis through "an international financial channel," indicating that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains on guard against potential economic impact from sharp market moves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN PM: NEED TO BE ALERT OVER DOWNSIDE RISKS OF YEN'S RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Wednesday that the government needs to be alert over possible downside risks posed by the yen's "one-sided rise" and reiterated that Tokyo will keep monitoring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements closely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER: TOO EARLY TO SUMMARIZE EFFECTS OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Finance Minister Jun Azumi said Wednesday that it is too early to summarize the effects of Tokyo's recent yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VICE FINANCE MINISTER: GLOBAL INFLATIONARY PRESSURES TO REMAIN HIGH IN 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China still faces many challenges, including rising protectionism, global inflationary pressures and the ongoing sovereign debt crisis in Europe, China Vice Finance Minister Li Yong said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA VICE FINANCE MINISTER: CHINA HAS STABILIZED PRICE INCREASES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has stabilized price increases and the economy is moving in the right direction, China Vice Finance Minister Li Yong said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA OFFICIAL: INFLATION RISKS SUCH AS EXCHANGE-RATE VOLATILITY REMAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Vice Finance Minister Shin Je-yoon said the government remains committed to price stability despite easing inflation in October, citing risks such as exchange-rate volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA PRIME MINISTER: HAVE TO GUARD AGAINST IMPORTED INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India needs to guard against imported inflationary pressure as the euro-zone continues to reel under the debt crisis, Prime Minister Manmohan Singh said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par FOCUS: NEXT UP, A RATE CUT IN KOREA? NOT LIKELY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Countries in the Asia-Pacific region are starting to cut interest rates to buttress their economies against a global slowdown. Despite easing inflation and slowing growth in South Korea, the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't likely to be among the next to ease policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INTERVIEW:FINNISH FIN MIN SEES NO ALERNATIVE FOR GREECE TO OCT 27 DEAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece has no alternative but to stick to a new bailout package agreed on October 27, and its membership of the euro zone would be an "open question" if voters were to reject that package, Finnish Finance Minister Jutta Urpilainen said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: PHILIPPINES OCTOBER CPI LIKELY UP 5.1% DUE TO TYPHOONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supply shocks in the Philippines caused by several typhoons likely spurred inflation to a quicker pace in October, although economists doubt that higher consumer prices would force a policy tightening from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid dimmer global economic growth prospects. [ 02-11-11 1106GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111102e7b2000x7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
