\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2011\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: GREEK CABINET APPROVES REFERENDUM PLAN, VOTE SEEN BY CHRISTMAS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece's cabinet Wednesday approved plans for a referendum on the country's latest bailout package, despite an open revolt by ruling-party lawmakers and amid fears the referendum could stoke further uncertainty in world financial markets. (published 0234 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW:FINNISH FIN MIN SEES NO ALERNATIVE FOR GREECE TO OCT 27 DEAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece has no alternative but to stick to a new bailout package agreed on October 27, and its membership of the euro zone would be an "open question" if voters were to reject that package, Finnish Finance Minister Jutta Urpilainen said Tuesday. (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMANY'S SCHAUEBLE EXPECTS GREECE TO EXECUTE REFORMS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said Greece's fellow euro-zone countries would lend their support should the country decide to implement the tough reforms agreed upon in the latest aid program, the German daily Financial Times Deutschland reported Wednesday. (published 0339 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ SHIRAI CONCERNED OVER IMPACT OF EURO CRISIS ON JAPAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan policy board member Sayuri Shirai said Wednesday that one of the most serious downside risks to the Japanese economy includes the negative effects of the European sovereign debt crisis through "an international financial channel," indicating that the bank remains on guard against potential economic impact from sharp market moves. (published 0310 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ SHIRAI: TO CONSIDER VARIOUS POLICY OPTIONS WITHIN MANDATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan policy board member Sayuri Shirai said Wednesday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will consider various options within its policy goal of achieving price stability, declining to directly comment on the possiblity of purchasing foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denominated assets. (published 0546 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM: NEED TO BE ALERT OVER DOWNSIDE RISKS OF YEN'S RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Wednesday that the government needs to be alert over possible downside risks posed by the yen's "one-sided rise" and reiterated that Tokyo will keep monitoring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements closely. (published 0509 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM NODA: KEEPING IN CONTACT WITH OTHER COUNTRIES ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Wednesday that his government is staying in touch with overseas authorities about global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, after Tokyo conducted yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier this week to curb its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's strength. (published 0215 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER: TOO EARLY TO SUMMARIZE EFFECTS OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Finance Minister Jun Azumi said Wednesday that it is too early to summarize the effects of Tokyo's recent yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0555 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VICE FINANCE MINISTER: GLOBAL INFLATIONARY PRESSURES TO REMAIN HIGH IN 2012 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China still faces many challenges, including rising protectionism, global inflationary pressures and the ongoing sovereign debt crisis in Europe, China Vice Finance Minister Li Yong said Wednesday. (published 0340 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA VICE FINANCE MINISTER: CHINA HAS STABILIZED PRICE INCREASES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has stabilized price increases and the economy is moving in the right direction, China Vice Finance Minister Li Yong said Wednesday. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA OFFICIAL: INFLATION RISKS SUCH AS EXCHANGE-RATE VOLATILITY REMAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Vice Finance Minister Shin Je-yoon said the government remains committed to price stability despite easing inflation in October, citing risks such as exchange-rate volatility. (published 0118 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: ECUADOR 2012 BUDGET PROPOSAL TOTALS $26.11 BLN - FIN MIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ecuador's government has proposed a $26.11 billion budget for next year, a 9% increase over the $23.95 billion plan it submitted for 2011, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said in a statement. (published 0442 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TAIWAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : 'IMPOSSIBLE' TO BE IMMUNE FROM GLOBAL SLOWDOWN AS EXPORTS FORM OVER 70% OF GDP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is impossible for Taiwan to be immune from a global economic slowdown as exports form 70% of the island's gross domestic product, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Perng Fai-nan told legislators Wednesday. (published 0315 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BANK INDONESIA OFFICIAL: ROOM FOR FURTHER RATE CUTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia sees room for further rate cuts after inflation eased in October, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Wednesday. (published 0148 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA'S ZHUHAI CITY IMPOSES CEILING ON HOME PRICES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zhuhai city in southern China's Guangdong province has set a ceiling of CNY11,285 per square meter for residential property transactions, becoming the first Chinese city to set a limit on residential property prices and triggering concerns of similar curbs in other cities. (published 0518 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA PRIME MINISTER: HAVE TO GUARD AGAINST IMPORTED INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India needs to guard against imported inflationary pressure as the euro-zone continues to reel under the debt crisis, Prime Minister Manmohan Singh said Wednesday. (published 0525 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par FOCUS: NEXT UP, A RATE CUT IN KOREA? NOT LIKELY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Countries in the Asia-Pacific region are starting to cut interest rates to buttress their economies against a global slowdown. Despite easing inflation and slowing growth in South Korea, the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't likely to be among the next to ease policy. (published 0412 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: PHILIPPINES OCTOBER CPI LIKELY UP 5.1% DUE TO TYPHOONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supply shocks in the Philippines caused by several typhoons likely spurred inflation to a quicker pace in October, although economists doubt that higher consumer prices would force a policy tightening from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid dimmer global economic growth prospects. (published 0504 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-11-11 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokum