\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2011\par 02:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The euro took a hit Monday as German leaders tempered hopes that an upcoming summit would lead to a deal resolving the continent's sovereign-debt crisis. Late Monday, the euro was at $1.3737, down more than one percent from $1.3884 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. It was at Y105.49, down 1.57% from Y107.18. The dollar was at Y76.83 from Y77.22 and CHF0.8988 from CHF0.8925. The U.K. pound ended the day at $1.5744 from $1.5828. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at about 77.186 from about 76.623.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by German warnings not to expect too much from Sunday's summit: Finance Minister Schaeuble says, "We will not have a definitive solution on the weekend." Merkel spokesman Steffen Seibert says it's an "impossible dream" to think the promised package will solve everything. EUR/USD also weighed by worries over euro-zone economic growth slowdown; expectations that private-sector bondholders will have to face deeper losses than those agreed in the July Greek debt-restructuring plan. But EUR/USD losses tempered by sovereign demand for euro. Data focus: 0900 GMT German October ZEW indicator of economic sentiment. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish-engulfing candlestick pattern completed yesterday. Support at 1.3723 (Friday's low), then at 1.3685 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.3582 (Wednesday's low), then 1.3565 (Oct. 11 low), 1.3346 (Oct. 10 low) and 1.3241 (Oct. 6 low). Resistance at 1.3806 (hourly chart), then at 1.3915 (yesterday's high); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, targeting 1.3936 (Sept. 15 high, near 55-day moving average), then 1.4079 (200-day moving average), 1.4120 (100-day moving average) and 1.4286 (Sept. 6 high).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with risks skewed lower. Undermined by capital flows to safe-haven yen and unwinding of yen-funded carry trades as risk aversion increases after Germany tempered expectations that comprehensive plan to end euro-zone financial crisis will be delivered at Sunday's EU summit. USD/JPY also weighed by lower U.S. Treasury yields, Japan exporter sales. But USD/JPY downside limited by Japan importer demand, expectations of Japan MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yen crosses vulnerable to 0200 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  3Q GDP (expected +9.2% on year). Data focus: 0000 GMT Fed's Evans speech, 1230 GMT U.S. September PPI, 1300 GMT U.S. August treasury international capital data, 1400 GMT U.S. October NAHB housing market index, 1715 GMT Fed Chairman Bernanke speech. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD in bullish mode, but stochastics turned bearish, 5- & 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Support at 76.60 (yesterday's low), then at 76.30 (Wednesday's low); breach would target 76.26-76.21 band (Sept. 27 low-Sept. 26 low), then 76.15-76.10 band (Sept. 23 low-Sept. 22 low) and 75.94 (Aug. 19 record low). Resistance at 77.46-77.49 (yesterday's high-Wednesday's high), then at 77.59 (Sept. 12 high); breach would target 77.86 (Sept. 9 high), then 78.18 (100-day moving average) and 78.48 (Aug. 8 high).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by increased investor risk aversion, ultra-loose BOE monetary policy. But GBP/USD losses tempered by GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus: 0830 GMT UK September inflation. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish-engulfing candlestick pattern completed yesterday. Support at 1.5719 (Friday's low), then at 1.5665 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.5541 (Wednesday's low), then 1.5525 (Oct. 10 low), 1.5421 (Oct. 7 low) and 1.5270 (Oct. 6 14-month low). Resistance at 1.5851 (Friday's high); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, targeting 1.5868 (Sept. 15 high), then 1.5944 (50% Fibonacci correction of 1.6618-1.5270 Aug. 19-Oct. 6 decline), 1.5965 (55-day moving average), 1.6067 (100-day moving average) and 1.6103 (61.8% correction).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with risks skewed higher. Buoyed by broadly stronger USD undertone as investor risk aversion grows; expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turned bullish at oversold; bullish-engulfing candlestick pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 0.9008 (Friday's high), then at 0.9039 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9115 (Wednesday's high), then 0.9125 (Oct. 11 high), 0.9290 (Oct. 10 high), 0.9317 (Oct. 6 high) and 0.9340 (April 1 high). Support at 0.8954 (hourly chart), then at 0.8882 (yesterday's low); breach would reinstate near-term negative bias, targeting 0.8858 (Sept. 21 low), then 0.8790 (Sept. 20 low), 0.8765 (200-day moving average) and 0.8646 (Sept. 15 low).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by heightened investor risk aversion, softer commodity & oil prices. USD/CAD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turned bullish at oversold; bullish key-reversal-day pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 1.0241 (yesterday's high), then at 1.0272 (Thursday's high); breach would target 1.0328 (Wednesday's high), then 1.0338 (Oct. 11 high), 1.0409-1.0417 band (Oct. 10 high-Oct. 7 high), 1.0482 (Oct. 6 high) and 1.0571 (Oct. 5 high). Support at 1.0123 (hourly chart), then at 1.0040 (yesterday's low); breach would reinstate near-term negative bias, targeting 1.0013 (55-day moving average), then 0.9855 (100-day moving average) and 0.9802 (200-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by intensified investor risk aversion, weaker commodity prices. But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest-rate differential, sovereign demand. Data focus: 0030 GMT October RBA monetary policy meeting minutes. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish-engulfing candlestick pattern completed yesterday. Support at 1.0142 (Friday's low), then at 1.0098 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9863 (Wednesday's low), then 0.9725 (Oct. 10 low), 0.9617 (Oct. 6 low) and 0.9484 (Oct. 5 low). Resistance at 1.0284 (hourly chart), then at 1.0371 (yesterday's high, near 200-day moving average); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, targeting 1.0398 (Sept. 16 high), then 1.0452 (100-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by elevated investor risk aversion, softer commodity prices. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics turned bearish at overbought; bearish-engulfing candlestick pattern completed yesterday. Support at 0.7903 (yesterday's low), then at 0.7887 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7739 (Wednesday's low), then 0.7655 (Oct. 10 low), 0.7620 (Oct. 6 low), 0.7550 (Oct. 5 low) and 0.7466 (Oct. 4 6-month low). Resistance at 0.8007 (hourly chart), then at 0.8068 (yesterday's high); breach would reinstate near-term positive bias, targeting 0.8131 (55-day moving average), then 0.8149 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of 0.8571-0.7466 Aug. 31-Oct. 4 decline) and 0.8218 (100-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan rose against the U.S. dollar late Monday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a lower dollar/yuan reference exchange rate on the back of a weaker dollar overnight Friday.\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3706 around 0830 GMT, down from CNY6.3785 late Friday. It traded between CNY6.3684 and CNY6.3780. The yuan has risen 7.2% against the U.S. unit since June 2010, when China effectively ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the dollar. Ahead of trading, the People's Bank of China fixed the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3710, down from Friday's 6.3762. Offshore, the yuan rose against the dollar in both the nondeliverable forwards and spot markets, tracking the onshore market. One-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards fell to 6.3760/6.3810 from 6.3990/6.4020 late Friday, implying a 0.1% fall by the yuan against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year. In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar-yuan exchange rate was at 6.4270 late Monday, down from 6.4425 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020111018e7ai0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
