\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan ends down, PBOC makes goodwill gesture before U.S. vote\b0\par\par\uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2011\par 12:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * PBOC fixing at peak before U.S. Senate votes on yuan bill\par \par \uc2 * Political gesture does not herald faster yuan rise\par \par \uc2 * Yuan seen reaching between 6.2-6.3/dollar by end of 2011\par \par \uc2 * Degree of appreciation seen within market expectations\par \par \uc2 * Yuan at 6.3750, up 3.37 pct so far this year\par \par \uc2 By Lu Jianxin and Jacqueline Wong\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, Oct 11 (Reuters) - The yuan reversed sharply against the dollar on Tuesday, scoring its biggest daily fall in nearly a year, brushing aside a record \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mid-point as the market expects no Chinese policy change towards a faster pace of yuan rises any time soon.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the reversal was normal consolidation after the yuan recorded its biggest single-day gain since its 2005 revaluation a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 "After the week-long holiday last week, exporters and importers were adjusting their positions, with yuan demand being quite strong yesterday while dollar demand\par \par \uc2 picked up today," said a trader at a Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "The situation should settle down in a few days, barring surprises driven by political factors. We are aware of rare volatility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days but see no other factors except the long holiday for now."\par \par \uc2 Among politically-related moves, the People's Bank of China fixed the yuan's daily mid-point at an all-time peak ahead of a vote by the U.S. Senate on a bill to press China to let the yuan appreciate faster.\par \par \uc2 The vote was delayed to Tuesday from last Thursday because of a dispute between Republicans and Democrats over the handling of amendments.\par \par \uc2 U.S. supporters of the bill say the Senate is almost certain to approve it. But the bill faces stronger opposition in the House where it may never face a vote. The White House has also voiced concerns that the legislation could violate international trade rules.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody understands the PBOC's fixing today is only a political goodwill gesture before the U.S. Senate votes on the bill," said a dealer at an Asian bank in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 "Nobody expects the war of words on the yuan will lead to faster yuan appreciation. Letting the yuan rise sharply for a short period of time is tactical and will not affect China's determination to manage the exchange rate on its own terms."\par \par \uc2 NO SURPRISE\par \par \uc2 Spot yuan closed at 6.3750 against the dollar, down 0.41 percent from 6.3486 at the close on Monday, its biggest single-day fall since early November last year.\par \par \uc2 The fall still lagged a 0.59 percent rally in the yuan versus the dollar on Monday, its biggest daily rise since its 2.1 percent revaluation in July 2005.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC set Tuesday's mid-point at a peak of 6.3483 versus the dollar, stronger than the fixing's historical high of 6.3586 on Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  uses the reference rate to signal the government's intentions for the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Spot yuan has now appreciated 3.37 percent since the start of this year and 7.08 percent since it was depegged from the dollar in June 2010.\par \par \uc2 Traders said they expected the yuan to reach somewhere between 6.2 and 6.3 against the dollar by the end of this year, meaning the yuan could rise about 5 to 6 percent for all 2011.\par \par \uc2 At these levels, the degree of yuan appreciation versus the dollar will be in line with forecasts made by many traders, economists and analysts at the start of this year.\par \par \uc2 China has bitterly criticised periodical moves by the United States to press it to let the yuan appreciate, saying such moves are politically motivated and dominated by domestic political needs in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese government has painted a picture of resisting U.S. calls for a stronger yuan, but in reality it has let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise -- at China's self-determined pace -- both to help adjust its economic structure so as to be less reliant on exports and in recognition of the importance of ties between the world's two biggest economies.\par \par \uc2 The government has entrusted the PBOC to control the pace of yuan appreciation via the daily mid-point from which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can rise or fall only 0.5 percent versus the dollar each day and via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading on the domestic market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 China Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System (CFETS).\par \par \uc2 Pressured by a weakening in the yuan in the onshore market, offshore benchmark one-year dollar/yuan non-deliverable forwards NDFs rose to 6.3830 in late trade from 6.3370 at the close on Monday.\par \par \uc2 They implied a minor yuan depreciation in 12 months compared with a minor appreciation they implied on Monday.\par \par \uc2 NDFs have become volatile since September amid a global dollar rally, which will trim the prospect for yuan appreciation.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA-YUAN-CLOSE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111014e7ab000o5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
