\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2011\par 15:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par OBAMA ASKS CONGRESS FOR $447B IN TAX CUTS, SPENDING \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama calls on Congress to pass a $447 billion package of spending initiatives and tax cuts to boost economic growth, in what might be the White House's last chance to change its political fortunes before the 2012 campaign kicks into high gear.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FLAGGING WORLD GROWTH TOPS G-7 AGENDA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heads from the Group of Seven leading developed economies will try to find agreed ways to rekindle weakening global economic growth when they meet later today.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN OUTLINES PROPOSALS TO COPE WITH YEN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's minister of economy, trade and industry released a list of proposals on how the government can cope with the strong yen, as had been requested by the prime minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES DOWN AMID OVERSEAS WEAKNESS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures move lower as weakness in overseas markets, a report of potentially large job cuts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  and some anxiety over a "credible" terror threat took some shine off President Obama's plan to spur job growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF AMERICA JOB CUTS MAY REACH 40,000 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  officials discuss cutting about 40,000 positions during a first wave of a restructuring, people familiar with the plans tell The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY'S SCHAEUBLE SAYS WORLD ECONOMY IN 'CRITICAL' PHASE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble says that using monetary policy is the wrong way to combat the economic crisis, but acknowledges that the world economy is now in a "critical" phase.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FITCH REITERATES WARNING OF BANK RISK TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATING \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings expects \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the country's banks, but warned again that this support "carries negative implications for the sovereign rating."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRUDE DOWN AS EURO SLUMPS, EUROPEAN STOCKS FALL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures are down as the euro slumps against the dollar and European stock markets fall on euro zone debt contagion worries and concerns the U.S. Federal Reserve is not doing enough to stimulate the U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU: IRELAND ON TRACK WITH DEFICIT-CUTTING PROGRAM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European authorities monitoring Ireland's bailout say the country is "well on track" to meet its deficit-cutting targets, thanks to a lower-than-expected cost of rescuing beleaguered Irish banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA INFLATION, GROWTH MODERATE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China reports production and price data for August that show moderate easing of both growth and inflation, bolstering confidence among some analysts that the government will be able to guide the world's second-largest economy to a soft landing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par Talk Back \par THE OBAMA-BERNANKE TAG TEAM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thomas Lam, group chief economist at OSK Group/DMG & Partners, notes that policy recommendations from Fed Chairman Bernanke and President Obama are not likely to sail through without hurdles and bumps.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Heard on the Street \par WEANING INSURERS OFF 9/11 SUPPORT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 9/11 attacks changed the world in many ways, not least for insurers who had to bear the cost. Legislation passed the following year handed the bulk of any bill for a future disaster onto the U.S. government. But there are steps that Washington could take to foster a viable private market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Analysis \par TRADERS RECALL PAINFUL MEMORIES OF SEPT. 11 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As Wall Street traders and their colleagues vividly recall Sept. 11, 2001, a decade's passing hasn't diminished the clarity of their memories as they recount events that significantly reshaped their lives and livelihoods.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interview \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP MORGAN\f28 \uc2 \f2  SAYS OBAMA MOVE SHOULD AVERT U.S. RECESSION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fresh measures promised by U.S. President Barack Obama to boost the economy should steer the country clear of recession, the global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at investment bank \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES SLIP AS OVERSEAS WEAKNESS OVERSHADOWS JOBS PLAN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures slip amidst weakness in overseas markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TREASURYS STEADY, RECOVER FROM EARLY LOSSES... \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US Treasurys are steady as European equities extend declines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO FALLS TO SIX-MONTH LOW AGAINST DOLLAR \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro falls to a six-month low against the dollar as equity markets slump.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRUDE DOWN AS EURO SLUMPS, EUROPEAN STOCKS FALL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures are down as the euro slumps against the dollar and equities fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GOLD SLIDES; INVESTOR CAUTION, STRONG DOLLAR WEIGH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold falls sharply as cautious sentiment and a strong dollar weighs on the metal. [ 09-09-11 1300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110909e799000zl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
