\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2011\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par FLAGGING WORLD GROWTH TOPS G-7 AGENDA IN MARSEILLES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heads from the Group of Seven leading developed economies will try to find agreed ways to rekindle weakening global economic growth when they meet in this port city later today.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN ANNOUNCES ROUGH PROPOSALS TO COPE WITH YEN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's minister of economy, trade and industry released a list of proposals on how the government can cope with the strong yen, as had been requested by the prime minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES LOWER AFTER OBAMA JOBS PROPOSAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock-index futures point to a lower open for Wall Street, with investors finding little solace from a jobs proposal by President Barack Obama, while a profit warning from chip maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Texas Instruments\f28 \uc2 \f2  also weighs on sentiment, strategists say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY'S SCHAEUBLE SAYS WORLD ECONOMY IN 'CRITICAL PHASE' \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble says that using monetary policy is the wrong way to combat the economic crisis, but acknowledges that the world economy is now in a "critical" phase."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FITCH REITERATES WARNING OF BANK RISK TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RATING \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings expects \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the country's banks, but warned again that this support "carries negative implications for the sovereign rating."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU: IRELAND ON TRACK WITH DEFICIT CUTTING PROGRAM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European authorities monitoring Ireland's bailout say the country is "well on track" to meet its deficit-cutting targets, thanks to a lower-than-expected cost of rescuing beleaguered Irish banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA INFLATION, GROWTH MODERATE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China reports production and price data for August that show moderate easing of both growth and inflation, bolstering confidence among some analysts that the government will be able to guide the world's second-largest economy to a soft landing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CANADA SHEDS 5,500 JOBS IN AUGUST \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada's job market lost a net 5,500 positions in August, and the jobless rate is up to 7.3% from 7.2% -- the first time this gauge of the unemployed rose in seven months. The jobless rate was expected to remain steady in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SWEDEN: GREEK DEFICITS WORSE THAN THOUGHT, CREDIBILITY KEY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sweden's Finance Minister Anders Borg says Greek deficits are worse than previously thought and urges lawmakers in Greece and Italy to continue to take steps to regain credibility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TULLOW, SHELL FIND OPENS NEW FRENCH GUIANA OIL PLAY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K.-listed oil companies Tullow and Shell say they have opened up a new hydrocarbon basin offshore French Guiana with the discovery of a good quality oil reservoir from their first wildcat well, sending Tullow's shares skyward.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par Interview \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP MORGAN\f28 \uc2 \f2  SAYS OBAMA MOVE SHOULD AVERT U.S. RECESSION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fresh measures promised by U.S. President Barack Obama to boost the economy should steer the country clear of recession, the global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at investment bank \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  says.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Watch \par SWISS FRANC GETS RELIEF; ACID TEST TO COME \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's move to set a ceiling for the franc versus the euro appears to have checked the Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's relentless rise for now, but the true test is yet to come, analysts say.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Focus \par ALL IS UP TO THE GREEKS NOW \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Greece wants to remain part of the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  now it will have to pay the price as European officials make it clear that no more concessions for saving the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its present form are in the offing, writes Nicholas Hastings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Sports \par US OPEN HEADS TO 4TH CONSECUTIVE MONDAY FINISH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All of the rain at the U.S. Open forces the tournament to change its schedule, pushing the men's final to Monday for the fourth consecutive year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES LOWER; LITTLE SOLACE FROM OBAMA JOBS PROPOSAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures lower with little solace from Obama's jobs proposal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TREASURYS STEADY, RECOVER FROM EARLY LOSSES... \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 US Treasurys are steady as European equities extend declines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO FALLS TO SIX-MONTH LOW AGAINST DOLLAR \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro falls to a six-month low against the dollar as equity markets slump.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CRUDE DOWN AS EURO SLUMPS, EUROPEAN STOCKS FALL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil futures are down as the euro slumps against the dollar and equities fall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPOT GOLD UP AS GROWTH WOES CONTINUE TO SUPPORT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold is higher in Europe as a dip in risk appetite drives buyers to perceived safe havens. [ 09-09-11 1200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110909e799000y2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
