\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2011\par 08:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN UNVEILS NEW FUND AND TIGHTENS REGULATIONS TO STEM YEN'S RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda on Wednesday launched a new two-prong initiative aimed at tackling the strong yen, creating a $100 billion fund to help spur investments by Japanese companies abroad and temporary reporting requirements for financial firms on their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S DOWNGRADES JAPAN DEBT RATING TO AA3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's Investors Service downgraded Japan's sovereign debt rating Wednesday, putting fresh pressure on the country's political leaders to repair its finances, the worst in the industrialized world.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S BYRNE: DON'T SEE JAPAN FUNDING CRISIS IN NEXT 12-18 MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan won't face the threat of a "funding crisis" over the next 12-18 months and a further credit rating downgrade is also unlikely over that period, despite the country's large budget deficit and a total debt load that is the highest of any major economy, a senior official at Moody's Investors Service said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DRAFT EFSF TREATY GIVES BROAD AUTHORITY TO EFSF DIRECTORS-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German finance minister Wolfgang Schaeuble has drawn up a framework for the future \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Europe's rescue fund that would grant considerable power to the fund's directors, German daily Handelsblatt reports Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FIN MIN: SHOULD ONLY TAKE STEPS AGAINST PROBLEMATIC CAPITAL FLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Wednesday warned that attempts to avoid crises with capital controls may stem inflows of desirable foreign money, arguing that regulatory measures should only be used to address capital flows that can disrupt economic fundamentals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOC'S MACKLEM SAYS G-20 FALLS SHORT IN CORRECTING IMBALANCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A top Canadian central banker said Wednesday the G-20 has fallen short in correcting global imbalances and that the spillover effects of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies are prompting other countries to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and adopt measures to curb capital inflows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA TRADE SURPLUS TO SHRINK FURTHER IN 2011 -MINISTRY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's trade surplus is likely to keep shrinking in 2011 and the jump in its July trade surplus was due to "chance," Chinese Commerce Ministry spokesman Shen Danyang said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA SHOULD STRENGTHEN, IMPROVE MACROECONOMIC CONTROLS - PAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should continue to strengthen and improve macroeconomic controls, and steady its policy direction as inflationary pressures remain, the People's Daily said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: CHINA MOVES CLOSER TO ACCEPTING REBEL TAKEOVER IN LIBYA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's government appeared to move closer to accepting a rebel takeover of Libya, praising the rebel leadership group in a statement Wednesday and saying it hoped the "new regime" will help restore stability in the war-torn North African nation. (published 0502 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S CREDIT VP SAYS QE3 STIMULUS MAY FAIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A so-called QE3 new round of "quantitative easing" where the U.S. Federal Reserve buys securities in large scale is unlikely to be enough to revive the world's largest economy, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  vice president and senior credit officer Steven Hess in an interview Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par HEARD ON THE ST: THE PRICE OF SECRECY FOR CENTRAL BANKS \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support got the world's banks through the 2008 crisis. But secrecy over the recipients of such largess make it a double-edged sword that can cause, as well as reduce, stress in the system. (published 1858 GMT) [ 24-08-11 0659GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110824e78o000fn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
