\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2011\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN UNVEILS NEW FUND AND TIGHTENS REGULATIONS TO STEM YEN'S RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda on Wednesday launched a new two-prong initiative aimed at tackling the strong yen, creating a $100 billion fund to help spur investments by Japanese companies abroad and temporary reporting requirements for financial firms on their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holdings. (published 0458 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 3RD UPDATE: MOODY'S DOWNGRADES JAPAN DEBT RATING TO AA3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's Investors Service downgraded Japan's sovereign debt rating Wednesday, putting fresh pressure on the country's political leaders to repair its finances, the worst in the industrialized world. (published 0151 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: MOODY'S BYRNE: DON'T SEE JAPAN FUNDING CRISIS IN NEXT 12-18 MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan won't face the threat of a "funding crisis" over the next 12-18 months and a further credit rating downgrade is also unlikely over that period, despite the country's large budget deficit and a total debt load that is the highest of any major economy, a senior official at Moody's Investors Service said Wednesday. (published 0340 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FIN MIN: SHOULD ONLY TAKE STEPS AGAINST PROBLEMATIC CAPITAL FLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Wednesday warned that attempts to avoid crises with capital controls may stem inflows of desirable foreign money, arguing that regulatory measures should only be used to address capital flows that can disrupt economic fundamentals. (published 0045 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOC'S MACKLEM SAYS G-20 FALLS SHORT IN CORRECTING IMBALANCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A top Canadian central banker said Wednesday the G-20 has fallen short in correcting global imbalances and that the spillover effects of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies are prompting other countries to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and adopt measures to curb capital inflows. (published 0405 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA TRADE SURPLUS TO SHRINK FURTHER IN 2011 -MINISTRY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's trade surplus is likely to keep shrinking in 2011 and the jump in its July trade surplus was due to "chance," Chinese Commerce Ministry spokesman Shen Danyang said Wednesday. (published 0550 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA SHOULD STRENGTHEN, IMPROVE MACROECONOMIC CONTROLS - PAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should continue to strengthen and improve macroeconomic controls, and steady its policy direction as inflationary pressures remain, the People's Daily said Wednesday. (published 0317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: CHINA MOVES CLOSER TO ACCEPTING REBEL TAKEOVER IN LIBYA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's government appeared to move closer to accepting a rebel takeover of Libya, praising the rebel leadership group in a statement Wednesday and saying it hoped the "new regime" will help restore stability in the war-torn North African nation. (published 0502 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: MOODY'S CREDIT VP SAYS QE3 STIMULUS MAY FAIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A so-called QE3 new round of "quantitative easing" where the U.S. Federal Reserve buys securities in large scale is unlikely to be enough to revive the world's largest economy, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  vice president and senior credit officer Steven Hess in an interview Wednesday. (published 0546 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: DAIRY EXPORTS UNDERPIN NEW ZEALAND'S JULY TRADE SURPLUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A strong rise in milk powder, butter and cheese exports and weaker-than-expected imports fueled New Zealand's first July trade surplus for 20 years. (published 0421 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN TOTAL CONSTRUCTION RISES 0.7% IN 2Q VS 1Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total value of construction in Australia, including building and engineering work, during the second quarter of 2011 rose 0.7% from the first quarter to A$42.49 billion, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said Wednesday. (published 0131 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  EXPECTS AUGUST INFLATION WITHIN 3.9%-4.8% RANGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas on Wednesday said it expects annual inflation in August to fall between 3.9% to 4.8%, a range that suggests a slowdown from the 4.6% in July. (published 0321 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par POSCO TO ISSUE KRW500B BONDS IN SEPTEMBER - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean steelmaker Posco plans to issue KRW500 billion of won-denominated bonds in the domestic market early next month to help finance its overseas resource development and steel business, a person familiar with the matter said Wednesday. (published 0303 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par HEARD ON THE ST: THE PRICE OF SECRECY FOR CENTRAL BANKS \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central-bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support got the world's banks through the 2008 crisis. But secrecy over the recipients of such largess make it a double-edged sword 