\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: 10-Year JGB Yield Tipped 0.970%-0.990% -Strategist\b0\par\par\uc2 2,202 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 01:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] The 10-year JGB yield may hover in a 0.970%-0.990% range, as investors await further cues on any possible JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, says Jun Ishii, chief strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, which weighs on Japan's export-driven economy, has fueled demand for safe-haven JGBs. But after the Nikkei reports Monday Japan may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , without coordination with central banks in other economies, to curb gains in the JPY, which hit a record high against the USD Friday, "players would like to take a wait-and-see approach on the government's emergency policies to deal with the strong yen and any additional monetary easing by the Bank of Japan," Ishii says. The 10-year yield was quoted at 0.980% at Friday's close. The lead September futures contract may trade in a 142.70-142.90 band compared with Friday's close at 142.77, Ishii says. "It will be difficult for JGBs to move much after the respite in U.S. Treasury gains at the end of last week despite the weak U.S. share markets," he adds. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi may fall after U.S. stocks end lower Friday on continuing worries over a potential global recession and the health of the European banking system, analysts say. Near-term support is tipped at 1700. Analysts expect the recent volatile trade to continue, while bargain hunting may lend some support after the index fell 6.2% to 1744.88 on Friday. How shipbuilders, autos and techs--the key export sectors--perform will likely be in focus as analysts cite the nation's high dependence on exports as a reason for the Kospi's recent underperformance; players will also take trading cues from whether foreigners extend their net selling streak. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2340 GMT [Dow Jones] LME base metals are likely to open mixed, with some cautious buying on depletion of inventories in some of the metals while weak global outlook may weigh on sentiment. Last week, Shanghai Futures Exchange copper inventory data showed a decline, the first in four weeks. China is one of the biggest consumers of base metals, and traders closely track the country's inventories, monetary policy and economic growth for signs of demand. Though most of the base metals ended higher Friday, they are trading at or below their nine-day moving average. LME 3-month copper ended 0.6% higher at $8,820/ton Friday, with nickel, the only loser, closing down 0.5% at $21,200. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says it is looking for copper to break over $9,000 to confirm upside toward $9,175. The house expects aluminum to be supported near $2,300 and says a move above $2,450 will be needed to confirm bullish traction. LME 3-month aluminum ended up 0.7% at $2,355/ton Friday. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at nine foreign brokerages placed net sell orders for 7.3 million Japan shares before the market open, according to traders. The 16th consecutive day of premarket selling may be negative for the market, although the yen value of the figures is unknown. Sell orders totaled 16.3 million shares, while buy orders amounted to 9.0 million shares. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2328 GMT [Dow Jones] The front-month Nymex crude contract fell 47 cents to $81.79 in early Asian trading Monday, as traders reacted to the prospect of an end to hostilities in Libya and an early resumption in crude oil exports. Rebels opposed to the rule of Col. Moammar Gadhafi moved into the capital Tripoli, meeting little resistance from forces loyal to the long-time ruler. Libya, an OPEC member, produced 1.65 million barrels of crude oil a day prior to civil war breaking out earlier this year. Oil exports have been suspended for several months, contributing to market tightness and a geopolitical risk premium made larger by popular protests in Syria. However, these supply bottlenecks have recently been overshadowed by worries over the global economy following a slew of downbeat economic data in the U.S. and sovereign debt issues in Europe. Nymex crude for September delivery settled 12 cents, or 0.2%, lower at $82.26/bbl Friday, in a session that saw prices dip as low as $79.17. ICE Brent crude closed $1.76 higher at $108.75/bbl. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei will likely be swayed by yen moves, as USD/JPY hit a record post-WWII low of 75.94 following the Friday Japan market close. Now at USD/JPY 76.79, the pair is under close scrutiny for government \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, while U.S. and European stock markets' high volatility continues to shake investor sentiment, says Takuya Yamada, senior portfolio manager at ITC Investment Partners. "At this level (for USD/JPY), we will still see funds flowing out of exporters and into domestic demand-related stocks such as \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO)," he says. He pegs today's Nikkei range at a relatively wide 8500-8900. Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 8630 vs the Osaka close at 8710. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Taiwan's industrial output in July likely grew 5.40% on year, faster than June's 3.61% rise, according to the median forecast of eight economists polled by Dow Jones. Economists say the growth was likely driven by the fading impact on supply-chain disruption due to Japan's March earthquake, but the weakening outlook of the U.S. and European markets and China's continued tighter policy could continue to weigh on the island's manufacturing activities. Although industrial output growth last month showed signs of stabilizing, "it is too early to assume smooth sailing for the global trade cycle just yet, especially for an island as exposed to U.S. consumers as Taiwan," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Donna Kwok. Raymond Yeung, an economist at ANZ, adds, the recent global economic downturn and worsening consumer sentiment could lead Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold off from raising benchmark rates. The data are due at 0800 GMT Tuesday. (jenny.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is higher on short-covering amid growing expectations the Bank of Japan may take fresh easing action after the pair hit a fresh record low of 75.94 Friday in New York, but sharper gains are unlikely as market participants await the Jackson Hole central bankers' summit starting Aug. 26, says a senior dealer at a major European bank in Tokyo. The USD/JPY is at 76.78 from 76.54 late Friday in New York. The pair may trade in a 76.50-77.00 band in Asia. Some market participants expect Japanese authorities may conduct JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the dealer doesn't expect any official moves, adding "75.50 is this week's target." Some market participants think \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not effective enough to reverse a broader market trend. As stocks continue to decline globally, the focus is on how Asian stocks will fare, he says. He tips the EUR/USD in a 1.4340-1.4400 range. The pair is at 1.4366. The EUR/JPY may trade in a 119.50-110.50 range, he says. The cross is at 110.35.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 2306 GMT incorrectly stated the date of the Jackson Hole summit. The correct version follows:) ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional markets are likely to fall after Wall Street took another leg downward Friday, marking the fourth straight week of losses, on continued concerns over a potential global recession and the European banking system. Tech stocks may fall after \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  dropped 20% to touch a six-year low on concerns over the world's largest personal-computer maker's extensive plans to reshape its business model. In the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the USD/JPY is likely to be a key focus after the USD/JPY touched a new record low Friday despite the near constant risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; the CHF is also likely to be closely watched on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns. The USD/JPY is at 76.50 from 76.54 late Friday in New York, after falling as low as 75.94. The EUR/USD is at 1.4396 from 1.4396, the EUR/JPY is at 110.12 from 110.20 and the USD/CHF is at 0.7849 from 0.7848. On the data slate, Thailand's 2Q GDP, Hong Kong's July CPI and Taiwan's jobless data are due. In the U.S., few reports are due and attention is likely to shift toward the annual conference hosted by the Kansas City Fed in Jackson Hole, Wyo., at the end of this week. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional markets are likely to fall after Wall Street took another leg downward Friday, marking the fourth straight week of losses, on continued concerns over a potential global recession and the European banking system. Tech stocks may fall after \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  dropped 20% to touch a six-year low on concerns over the world's largest personal-computer maker's extensive plans to reshape its business model. In the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the USD/JPY is likely to be a key focus after the USD/JPY touched a new record low Friday despite the near constant risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; the CHF is also likely to be closely watched on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns. The USD/JPY is at 76.50 from 76.54 late Friday in New York, after falling as low as 75.94. The EUR/USD is at 1.4396 from 1.4396, the EUR/JPY is at 110.12 from 110.20 and the USD/CHF is at 0.7849 from 0.7848. On the data slate, Thailand's 2Q GDP, Hong Kong's July CPI and Taiwan's jobless data are due. In the U.S., few reports are due and attention is likely to shift toward the annual conference hosted by the Kansas City Fed in Jackson Hole, Wyo., at the end of next week. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 21 Aug 2011 19:44 EDT DJ  MARKET TALK: 10-Year JGB Yield Tipped -2-\par \par \uc2 2301 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index faces broadly negative offshore leads. SPI 200 futures rose 0.1% to 4072.0, the S&P 500 fell 1.5% to 1123.53 and spot gold hit a record high of US$1,880.00 before retreating to US$1,853.40. U.S. 10-year bond yields remained near record lows, at 2.07%. Wall Street's fall was exaggerated by steep falls in \uc2 Hewlett Packard\uc2  and IBM, which accounted for more than half the 1.6% fall in the DJIA. Over the weekend, EU and German leaders hardened their opposition to euro-zone bonds. But on a slight positive note for risk assets, Japan's Nikkei newspaper says Japan was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against yen strength, and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Xinhua quotes \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's president saying China and the U.S. should jointly boost the global economic recovery. S&P 500 Financials fell 1.9%, Materials fell 1.4%, Industrials fell 1.5% and Energy fell 1.7%. Nymex crude oil fell 12 cents to US$82.26. LME copper fell 0.6%. BHP (BHP.AU) ADRs fell to A$37.38 vs A$37.50 locally; the S&P/ASX 200 closed at 4101.9 on Friday. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Thailand's economic growth likely decelerated in 2Q as the adverse effect from supply disruptions in the auto industry took its toll. According to the median forecast of seven economists polled by Dow Jones, 2Q GDP likely grew 0.4% on-quarter vs a 2.0% rise in 1Q. On year-on-year basis, 2Q GDP likely rose 3.55% vs a 3.0% rise in 1Q. As Japanese auto makers resumed their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pent-up orders will likely to result in ramped up production in 2H. The clear and present threat for the Thai economy in 2H is the economic hardship in the U.S. and debt problems in the Euro zone. "We need to closely monitor the situation out there in the West. We are hearing more and more bad news on their economy and the material impact on our economy will likely to be felt more in the second half of this year," says Pornthep Jubandhu, a macroeconomic analysis manager at \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2 . The NESDB is expected to release the 2Q GDP data at 0230 GMT Monday. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 21, 2011 19:44 ET (23:44 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110821e78l003oa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
