\par\par \b \uc2 Ex-PBOC Adviser: Must Stop  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\uc2  To Ease  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserve Accumulation\b0\par\par\uc2 230 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2011\par 10:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--\uc2 The People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  must stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if it wants to ease its accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which is a significant factor behind domestic inflation, former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser Yu Yongding said in a commentary in Caixin Century magazine.\par \par \uc2 The size of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is "far beyond" reasonable, and "the most important thing for China is to stop adding to them," Yu said.\par \par \uc2 A floating exchange rate could help buffer external shocks while enabling China to keep monetary policy independent, Yu said. Yuan appreciation could also help the government achieve its target of keeping inflation under 4% this year.\par \par \uc2 "Pegging the yuan to the U.S. dollar can't guarantee yuan stability as the dollar has been fluctuating strongly against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Yu said.\par \par \uc2 Yu said China will inevitably experience a short-term decline in exports and increased unemployment if the authorities allow the yuan to float, "but macroeconomic conditions are conducive to significant yuan appreciation, and the government will help companies and workers pull throw."\par \par \uc2 To prevent excessive appreciation, tightening capital controls and strictly combating speculative money inflows are necessary, Yu said.\par \par \uc2 