\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Current Events Give Opportunity To Boost Yuan Flexibility\b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2011\par 04:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- China state scholar says current events provide opportunity to boost flexibility in the yuan\par \par \uc2 -- China should consider "manufacturing" two-way volatility in the yuan, scholar says\par \par \uc2 -- China may reduce required reserve ratio in second half of the year, scholar says\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments on banks' reserve ratio requirement and interest rates in the final two paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 By Aaron Back\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China should use current turmoil in international markets as an opportunity to boost the flexibility of its exchange rate, a Chinese government scholar said in an essay distributed Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "We should not re-peg the yuan to the dollar again as soon as an international financial crisis breaks out," said Zhang Ming, a scholar at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, a top state think tank.\par \par \uc2 "After all, the U.S. debt ceiling crisis and the U.S. credit downgrade have already forced us to rethink the traditional policy of acquiring \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in order to maintain the stability of the exchange rate," he said.\par \par \uc2 To the extent that international financial turmoil may effect the outlook for the Chinese economy, it may also change expectations for the direction of the yuan, Zhang said.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, China should take advantage of the current situation to increase the flexibility of the exchange rate by widening the daily trading band to 1.0 percentage above or below the daily fixing rate, compared to 0.5% now, he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should also consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to "manufacture" two-way movement in the yuan exchange rate, breaking the one-way expectations for yuan appreciation, he said.\par \par \uc2 Zhang said that China should pause on hikes to the required reserve ratio--the level of deposits that banks must hold in reserve with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He also said China shouldn't rule out cutting the ratio in the second-half of 2011 to give smaller companies better access to credit.\par \par \uc2 However there is no justification currently for cutting interest rates, as inflation remains high and real deposit rates are still clearly negative, Zhang said.\par \par \uc2 -By Aaron Back, Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7701; aaron.back@dowjones.com [ 11-08-11 0233GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110811e78b0007e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
