\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Weaker, SNB Fails To Cool Red-Hot Franc\b0\par\par\uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 August 2011\par 13:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --SNB efforts to tame Swiss franc strength prove short-lived\par \par \uc2 --Nervous calm prevails after Fed pledges to keep rates at ultra-low levels until mid-2013\par \par \uc2 --Choppy morning for the pound as BOE delivers quarterly inflation report\par \par \uc2 --China fixes dollar at a record-low against the yuan\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Clare Connaghan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Fresh efforts by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  to cool the white-hot Swiss franc had little impact Wednesday, while the dollar was mostly weaker after the Federal Reserve said U.S. interest rates will probably stay at ultra-low levels for at least another two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the franc scaled fresh record highs overnight against the dollar and euro, the SNB announced that it will expand banks' most readily available deposits from CHF80 billion ($111 billion) to CHF120 billion and conduct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap transactions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest measures fell short of direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and failed to reverse the tide of franc appreciation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro initially spiked to CHF1.0529 after the news, before falling back to trade just above CHF1.04 by 1053 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The SNB needs a big stick of dynamite to turn the trend [of franc appreciation] around and I don't think these measures are it," said Paul Mackel, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the pound had a choppy morning after the Bank of England cut its forecasts for U.K. economic growth and inflation, signaling that there is little prospect of a change in monetary policy in either direction in the coming months. The pound initially came under pressure against the dollar and fell to a one-month low against the euro before recovering ground against both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "All-in-all, the Bank of England's August quarterly inflation report was a somber document, laden with warnings about the various headwinds confronting the British economy, the euro-zone crisis, the U.S. slowdown, supply chain disruption caused by the Japanese disaster and lack of consumer confidence," said Nick Beecroft of Saxobank.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By 1053 GMT the pound was at $1.6257 against the dollar, compared with $1.6307 late Tuesday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More broadly, dollar weakness persisted in European hours after Fed officials signaled Tuesday that they were committed to keeping rates at their current, exceptionally low levels "at least through mid-2013," suggesting policymakers will continue to support efforts to lift the struggling U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 They also said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had discussed a "range of policy tools" that it was "prepared to employ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Australia, Canada and New Zealand held onto gains made in Asia against the dollar, while the safe-haven yen neared pre-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels against the buck.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In emerging markets, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the dollar at a record-low fixing against the yuan, with the central parity rate at CNY6.4167. The zloty came under pressure against the euro, while the forint bounced off session lows against the 17-country \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ahead of the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  July rate meeting minutes due at 1200 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1048 GMT, the euro was trading at $1.43844 against the dollar, compared with $1.4373 late Tuesday in New York, according to trading system EBS. The dollar was at Y76.54 against the yen, compared with Y76.96, while the euro was at Y110.090 compared with Y110.55.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 73.86 compared with 73.91 late Tuesday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A summary of key levels for chart-watching technical strategists is below:\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       EUR/USD    USD/JPY    GBP/USD    USD/CHF \par  \par Spot 1046 GMT     1.4386     76.53      1.6259     0.7258 \par 3 Day Trend       Range      Bearish    Range      Bearish \par Weekly Trend      Range      Bearish    Bullish    Bearish \par 200 day ma        1.4537     81.78      1.6091     0.8945 \par 3rd Resistance    1.4432     77.45      1.6410     0.7480 \par 2nd Resistance    1.4432     77.05      1.6326     0.7405 \par 1st Resistance    1.4402     76.70      1.6283     0.7332 \par Pivot*            1.4302     77.17      1.6297     0.7290 \par 1st Support       1.4310     76.29      1.6191     0.7184 \par 2nd Support       1.4285     76.25      1.6176     0.7067 \par 3rd Support       1.4195     75.68      1.6131     0.6985 \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       EUR/CHF \par  \par Spot 1046 GMT     1.0441 \par 3 Day Trend       Bearish \par Weekly Trend      Bearish \par 200 day ma        1.2517 \par 3rd Resistance    1.0841 \par 2nd Resistance    1.0620 \par 1st Resistance    1.0530 \par Pivot*            1.0424 \par 1st Support       1.0285 \par 2nd Support       1.0075 \par 3rd Support       1.0022 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Clare Connaghan, Dow Jones Newswires; +44 (0) 20 7842 9496, clare.connaghan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jessica Mead and Dow Jones Technical Strategist Francis Bray contributed to this article.) [ 10-08-11 1129GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110810e78a0019y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
