\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: China Researcher Urges Government To Sell Dollar Assets\b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2011\par 03:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Chinese state researcher says U.S. will ultimately have to print money to deal with deficits\par \par \uc2 -- Recommends China reduce dollar-denominated assets as soon as possible\par \par \uc2 -- Also lays out long-term plan for a new \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization fund\par \par \uc2 (adds plans for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization fund in sixth to 12th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Aaron Back and Rose Yu \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China should reduce its dollar-denominated assets as soon as possible to safeguard its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, a government researcher said Thursday, as the impasse over raising the U.S. debt limit dragged on in Washington.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Zhang Monan, a researcher at the State Information Center, a central government think tank, said in a commentary published in the state-run Shanghai Securities News that U.S. government debt and the dollar have lost their long-term stability.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The higher the U.S. debt level, the bigger impact it will have on the stability of China's economy," she wrote in the piece.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the face of its sky-high deficits, the U.S. will have to continue to print money to let the dollar depreciate, and a third round of asset purchases, known as quantitative easing, will be inevitable, said Zhang.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator, the State Administration of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has come under considerable criticism from academics and other observers for its management of the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, which at the end of June reached $3.2 trillion, the largest of any country in the world. Market watchers estimate U.S. dollar assets account for as much as two-thirds of the total.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a separate column in another Chinese newspaper, Zhang also proposed a long-term reform of how China manages its exchange rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under the current system, in order to maintain the desired yuan exchange rate, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buys up most of the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that enters the country, issuing yuan in return, which swells the domestic money supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To change this, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should set up a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stabilization fund that only \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets when the yuan exchange rate goes beyond certain target ranges, similar to how Japan manages the yen exchange rate, she said. Proposals for this kind of fund have been made by many Chinese scholars in recent months, but the key question of how to finance the stabilization fund remains unanswered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In order to avoid increasing the domestic money supply, the fund would likely have to be set up using general revenues in the coffers of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rather than using the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  balance sheet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Writing in the Securities Times, Zhang said her proposed fund would be ultimately controlled by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but administered by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However she was not clear how the mechanism would be funded, saying only that "the government would endow the fund with the necessary amount of domestic and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Newspaper website: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.cnstock.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.cnstock.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Newspaper website: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.stcn.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.stcn.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Aaron Back and Rose Yu, Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7701; aaron.back@dowjones.com [ 28-07-11 0127GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110728e77s0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
