\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: PBOC Professor Joins Chorus Against Overly Fast Yuan Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2011\par 10:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- PBOC professor joins critics of faster yuan rise, says China needs to contain appreciation expectations\par \par \uc2 -- PBOC professor says China should abandon view that rising yuan will subdue inflation\par \par \uc2 -- PBOC professor says \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs "stable approach" to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when appropriate\par \par \uc2 (Recasts 1st paragraph, adds background in 5th-7th paragraphs and 13th-14th paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--A professor at a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  training center added his voice Thursday to the growing chorus of criticism against the yuan's rapid climb, amid concerns the Chinese economy may slow more dramatically than expected.\par \par \uc2 Wang Yong, a professor at the People's Bank of China training institute in Zhengzhou, Henan province, said in an opinion piece that China must contain market expectations of yuan appreciation to prevent damage to China's economy if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bubble bursts.\par \par \uc2 "If the yuan appreciation bubble pops, China's economy and financial markets would see a severe impact," Wang wrote in the state-run Shanghai Securities News.\par \par \uc2 The yuan hit a high of 6.4605 to the dollar on June 23 after the one-year anniversary of China's depegging of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the U.S. dollar. That represented a 5.7% rise from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 China has pledged to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen to help contain inflation and facilitate a long-planned shift in the economy away from exports and toward increased consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 But criticism of the yuan's gains has mounted as disappointing economic data has fueled concerns the fastest growing major economy in the world may be facing a "hard landing."\par \par \uc2 The PBOC has continued to allow the yuan to rise even as the dollar has strengthened in the past two months. The yuan is up 0.4% against the dollar since May 1, while the ICE dollar index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has risen 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 A number of state economists have called for caution in yuan appreciation to avoid asset bubbles from hot money inflows.\par \par \uc2 A rapidly rising yuan in the near term would offer evidence that global speculators are pumping up a bubble, Wang wrote. "And if that's the case, there's a real danger that the same speculators may pop the yuan bubble in three to six months."\par \par \uc2 China should take a "stable approach" to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms, especially in terms of the pace of appreciation, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as appropriate, Wang said.\par \par \uc2 "We must abandon the view that a rising yuan will help alleviate imported inflationary pressure. It works in theory but in reality, this point of view is not suitable for the current state of the economy," Wang said.\par \par \uc2 The yuan may become overvalued if appreciation accelerates, Zhang Monan, a researcher with the State Information Center, wrote Tuesday in the state-run Economic Information Daily.\par \par \uc2 Another influential researcher, Xiang Songzuo, deputy director of the International Monetary Research Institute at Renmin University of China, said in a recent interview the yuan could reverse course next year in a climate of political caution as candidates jockey for position ahead of a reshuffle of top Communist Party and government jobs.\par \par \uc2 -By Jean Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; jean.yung@dowjones.com [ 30-06-11 0829GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110630e76u000nc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
