\par\par \b \uc2 TIMELINE -China's reforms of yuan exchange rate regime\b0\par\par\uc2 862 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2011\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING, June 17 (Reuters) - China will continue to make the yuan more flexible to better reflect market forces, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  said in its annual financial stability report this week that further yuan regime reform will help curb inflation and asset bubbles.\par \par \uc2 Chinese leaders, including Premier Wen Jiabao, have long pledged to forge ahead with market-based reforms of the yuan regime, but they have ruled out another one-off revaluation.\par \par \uc2 As the one-year anniversary of the yuan's de-pegging from the dollar approaches, speculation is rife that China could unveil fresh steps to overhaul its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, including a widening of the yuan's daily trading band against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Standard Chartered Bank said in a note earlier this month that China may widen the yuan's trading band to 1 percent from the current 0.5 percent "over the next few weeks".\par \par \uc2 In July 2005, China revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent and abandoned a decade-old dollar peg, and allowed the yuan to gain 19 percent in the following three years.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then effectively re-pegged the yuan to the dollar again at 6.83 in July 2008 to counter the global financial crisis. On June 19, 2010, it unshackled the yuan from the peg and said it would make the yuan "more flexible".\par \par \uc2 Here is a timetable of yuan reforms.\par \par \uc2 1988 -- China sets up semi-official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap centres around the country to allow enterprises to trade the yuan at a rate that more closely reflects market demand.\par \par \uc2 1994, Jan. 1 - China unifies its dual exchange rates by bringing the official and swap centre rates into line, officially devaluing the yuan by 33 percent overnight to 8.7 to the dollar as part of reforms to embrace a "socialist market economy".\par \par \uc2 April - China sets up its first interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 China Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System -- in Shanghai. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the yuan stable.\par \par \uc2 1996, Dec - China allows the yuan to be fully convertible under the current account.\par \par \uc2 1994-1996 -- The yuan strengthens steadily from 8.7 to the dollar to around 8.28.\par \par \uc2 1997-1999 -- China wins wide praise for keeping the yuan stable during the Asian financial crisis despite pressure to devalue. The yuan was boxed between 8.2770 and 8.2800 for about three years through frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2000 -- The yuan is allowed to close slightly outside the firm end of its 30-basis point band, which is later widened by another 10 points to 8.2760-8.2800 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 2001, December -- China joins the World Trade Organisation and pledges to adjust its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime gradually.\par \par \uc2 2003 -- International pressure begins mounting for the yuan to appreciate to help balance global trade, including China's huge trade surplus with the United States and the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 2004, Dec. 8 -- Premier Wen Jiabao says that China would move gradually to a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.\par \par \uc2 2005, July 21 -- China revalues the yuan by 2.1 percent and revises the rules governing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, saying it had shifted to "a managed floating exchange rate based on market supply and demand with reference to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 -- The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the dollar , euro\par \par \uc2 , yen and Korean won are the main \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its reference basket when managing the yuan's exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Others include the Singapore dollar , sterling , the Malaysian ringgit , Russian rouble , Australian dollar , Thai baht and Canadian dollar . The weightings are secret.\par \par \uc2 2005 July - 2008 July -- The yuan appreciated steadily, rising 19 percent against the dollar accumulatively. China's trade surplus and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves kept rising in the same period.\par \par \uc2 2005 September - China widened the daily trading band between yuan and non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including euro, yen and Hong Kong dollar, to 3 percent from the previous 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 2007 May -- China widened the yuan's daily trading band against the dollar to 0.5 percent from 0.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 2008, July -- China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively pegs the yuan against the dollar close to 6.83 to protect China's economy as it confronted a slowdown due to the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 2010, June -- China said it was going to resume reforming the yuan exchange rate and increase \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility after keeping the yuan tightly linked to the U.S. dollar for nearly two years during and after the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 2010, August - China starts to trade the yuan against the Malaysia ringgit in the onshore market.\par \par \uc2 2010, November - China starts to trade the yuan against the Russian rouble in the onshore market.\par \par \uc2 2011, April - China allowed the yuan to break the psychologically important 6.5 per dollar mark.\par \par \uc2 2011, July - The yuan hit a record high of 6.4634 per dollar, extending its gains so far this year to 1.9 percent and 5.6 percent since the depeg in June 2010.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Zhou Xin and Kevin Yao; Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-YUAN/TIMELINE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110620e76h000aa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
