\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: PBOC Official:  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Should Set Up Fund For  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 511 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2011\par 10:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should set up new a fund or use fiscal receipts for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , PBOC official and adviser say\par \par \uc2 -- Current policy swells money supply, fuels inflation\par \par \uc2 -- PBOC adviser says yuan stability is one goal among many for government\par \par \uc2 (Recasts 1st paragraph, adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser Zhou Qiren's comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 3rd-4rth paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should set up a new fund or use fiscal receipts to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than printing yuan to buy up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a PBOC official and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adviser wrote in separate commentaries Monday.\par \par \uc2 To maintain capital controls and keep a grip on the yuan exchange rate, the PBOC issues yuan to buy up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  that enters the country, a process that swells the domestic money supply and exacerbates China's inflation problem, Xu Nuojin, deputy governor of the People's Bank of China's Guangzhou branch, wrote in an article in the PBOC-backed Financial News.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Zhou Qiren, an adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in an article on his personal website Monday that China should allocate some fiscal resources, such as fiscal revenue, national bonds or gains from state-owned assets, to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Maintaining yuan stability is just one of the government's many goals, Zhou said, ranking a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  alongside education, health and infrastructure development. The country should allocate some fiscal resources to each goal, he added.\par \par \uc2 Neither Xu nor Zhou have authority over national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but their views are representative of those held by many top analysts and policy makers in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Proposals like Xu's for a separate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund have been floated in recent months by Chinese academics and officials. Experts say that if the fund were financed by issuing new yuan it would have the same effect on money supply as the current system, but such a fund could be financed through other means, such as general tax revenues. Xu didn't give further details as to how such a fund would be set up or financed.\par \par \uc2 Xu added that China could consider diversifying its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves away from U.S. dollar assets by cutting the share of reserves held in the form of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds, while adding resources, equities and energy and metals that are of strategic importance to China.\par \par \uc2 Many Chinese analysts have called for China to invest more of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in areas like precious metals and commodities to diversify away from U.S. dollar holdings as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens. But others, including PBOC Vice Gov. Yi Gang, who heads the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have argued that it is difficult for China to do so without having a large impact on the prices of those commodities.\par \par \uc2 -Stefanie Qi and Yajun Zhang contributed to this article; Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7331; stefanie.qi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 13, 2011 04:10 ET (08:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110613e76d001ci\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
