\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(6/14) Beijing Reels In Bank Lending\b0\par\par\uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2011\par 20:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Jason Dean \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BEIJING -- China reported a sharp slowdown in credit growth in May, but expectations of another high reading for inflation prompted analysts to predict still more tightening ahead for the world's No. 2 economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Chinese banks issued 551.6 billion yuan ($85.1 billion) of new loans in May, down from 739.6 billion yuan in April and below economists' expectations of 650 billion yuan, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed Monday.\par \par \uc2 The credit number was the latest sign of slowing activity in China's economy -- a major source of global growth -- after a series of interest-rate increases and other measures aimed at taming consumer prices. Surveys of purchasing managers have indicated weakening activity, and housing prices have started falling in some big cities. A drop in housing prices could cool activity in construction, which has propelled China's expansion and fed demand for commodities such as iron ore and copper.\par \par \uc2 Yet broad inflation remains worrisomely high. China's National Bureau of Statistics was scheduled Tuesday to report a raft of other economic data for May, including the consumer price index. Economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires forecast it will show a 5.5% increase from a year earlier. That would outpace the recent high of 5.4%, hit in March, and mark the fastest clip since the index rose 6.3% in July 2008.\par \par \uc2 Lu Ting, China economist for \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 , expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep controlling lending and to raise benchmark interest rates once more this year. He said the May \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed that the focus remains on inflation. "\uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has kept tightening monetary policy, which was set at the beginning of this year, despite recent signs of an economic slowdown," he said.\par \par \uc2 In a note after the data Monday, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 's Yu Song and Helen Qiao said, "Tightening is likely to continue at least in June."\par \par \uc2 Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures also showed that China's broadest measure of money supply, M2, was up 15.1% at the end of May from a year earlier, lower than the 16.6% rise at the end of April, and below economists' expectations for a 15.5% rise.\par \par \uc2 China's benchmark stock index has fallen nearly 12% since mid-April as a result of jitters about a slowdown. The market largely took Monday's data in stride, though, with the Shanghai Composite Index settling down 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 One factor feeding inflation has been inflows of funds from China's exports or foreign investment. Because of its controlled exchange rate, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must purchase that foreign cash, which it does by issuing new yuan -- a mechanism that adds to inflation by increasing the money supply.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official and an adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  separately called for the government to change that practice to avoid increasing the money supply.\par \par \uc2 Xu Nuojin, deputy head of the PBOC's Guangzhou branch, wrote in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -backed Financial News that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should set up a new fund to conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than printing yuan to buy up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Zhou Qiren, an adviser to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in an article on his personal website that China should allocate some fiscal resources, such as fiscal revenue, national bonds or gains from state-owned assets, to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Neither Mr. Xu nor Mr. Zhou has authority over national \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but their views suggest a debate among top analysts and policy makers about how to adapt China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system to soaring prices.\par \par \uc2 Separately on Monday, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator said it will relax certain rules on banks' conversion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for their own business \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as of July 1, a further incremental easing of the government's grip on the still tightly controlled capital account.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Liu Li and Stefanie Qi contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 13, 2011 14:21 ET (18:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110613e76d0044m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
