\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,152 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2011\par 12:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN EMPLOYERS URGE PRIVATE SECTOR PARTICIPATION IN GREECE-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The President of the German Employers' Association agrees with the government's proposal to involve private-sector bondholders in further financial help for Greece, according to an article set to be published by Hamburger Abendblatt Tuesday. (published 0902 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN'S SOCIALIST CONSIDER CALLING EARLY ELECTIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some top officials in the Spanish government, and elsewhere in the ruling Socialist party, are pushing Prime Minister Jose Luis Rodriguez Zapatero to call elections in November, well ahead of the March, 2012 deadline, El Pais reports, citing people close to the situation. (published 0815 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALY APRIL INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT SURGES 1.0% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian industrial output surged in April, driven by durable consumer goods, national statistics office Istat said Monday. (published 0908 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN CORE MACHINERY ORDERS POST SURPRISE DECLINE OF 3.3% IN APRIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese core machinery orders posted an unexpected 3.3% drop from a month earlier in April on the back of a decline in demand from manufacturers, the Cabinet Office said Monday. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MAY NEW YUAN LOANS DROP, MONEY SUPPLY GROWTH SLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese banks extended fewer new yuan loans in May, and money supply growth slowed, showing the impact of monetary tightening in the world's second-largest economy. (published 0338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA SCHOLAR: MUST ELIMINATE ONE-WAY BET ON YUAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China needs to eliminate expectations of a steady appreciation of the yuan and avoid a one-way bet on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that has attracted "hot money," an influential academic said. (published 0223 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OECD GURRIA: CONCERTED POLICY DECISION BY CHINA TO EASE GROWTH PACE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese authorities are making concerted efforts to cool the country's economic growth, Angel Gurria, the Secretary General of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development, said Monday. (published 0557 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: CHINA SHOULD SET UP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FUND TO CURB INFLATION \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should set up a stabilization fund to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to keep the yuan's exchange rate within a target range, a move that would curb inflation, a local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official wrote in a commentary piece on Monday. (published 0542 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA OFFICIAL: ASSET BUBBLE RISKS, INFLATION PUT PRESSURE ON HK ECONOMIC HEALTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks of an asset bubble and rising inflation are putting pressure on the health of Hong Kong's financial system and its economy, a senior Chinese official warned Monday. (published 0525 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: SHOULD USE FISCAL RECEIPTS TO MAINTAIN STABLE YUAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should rely more on fiscal receipts rather than printing money to maintain the stability of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Zhou Qiren, an adviser to the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said in an article on his personal website Monday. (published 0517 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA INTEREST RATE HIKE NEEDED AS PRICES RISE - PBOC-BACKED NEWSPAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another interest rate hike in China is still needed as food and non-food prices continue to rise, a commentary piece in a newspaper backed by China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  argued Monday. (published 0026 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA WELL POISED TO SUSTAIN HIGH ECONOMIC GROWTH - FINANCE MINISTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India is poised to sustain a high rate of economic expansion for some years as the country has the growth drivers in place, despite signals that the pace of factory output growth is weakening, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said Monday. (published 0523 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA FIN MIN: TO RUN MACRO POLICIES SUCH AS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , POLICY RATE 'FLEXIBLY' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan said Monday that the authorities will continue to prioritize price stability when operating the macroeconomic policies. (published 2357 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA BANKS' SHORT-TERM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  BORROWING ROLLOVER RATE UP IN MAY VS APRIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The proportion of short-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  borrowings rolled over by South Korean banks rose substantially in May from the previous month but fell short of fully replacing maturing debt, the Financial Supervisory Service said Monday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TAIWAN SEEKS UP TO 25-YEAR JAIL TERM IN FOOD SCARE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TAIPEI --Taiwan prosecutors on Monday sought jail terms of up to 25 years for four people on charges of selling banned chemicals to food makers, triggering the island's worst food scare in decades. (published 1040 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: SLUGGISH HIRING SEEN AS A THREAT TO US RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The potential for a persistent slowdown in hiring is the biggest threat to the U.S. recovery, according to economists in the latest Wall Street Journal economic forecasting survey, as they sharply cut the number of jobs they projected the economy would create in coming months. (published 0411 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STRONG AFTERSHOCKS RATTLE NEW ZEALAND'S CHRISTCHURCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's second largest city Christchurch was hit by several strong aftershocks Monday, causing further damage to the already earthquake-devastated city. (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR UNDER THE COSH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Renewed EUR bear pressure on support at 1.4285 is expected, as the powerful downtrend off the June 7 reaction high at 1.4696 looks to extend. The 1.618 Fibonacci extension target at 1.4265 will provide some support, to protect the next layer of support at 1.4135. Corrective gains are limited to the 1.4445 area, and only above there would lift the bearish EUR outlook. (published 0701 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN COMMODITY TECHNICALS: DOWNSIDE RISK INCREASING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday Brent : The setback off Friday's high at $120.07 a barrel is threatening to extend below $117.63. A head-and-shoulders top pattern on the 60-minute chart threatens to mark the peak of a five-day bull wave, and prompt a deeper setback towards the $115.65 area. A recovery above $119.70 protects the $120.07 high, but only above the latter would create room for the $120.95 wave equality target. (published 0820 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN EQUITY INDEX TECHNICALS: BEAR TREND REMAINS INTACT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday FTSE 100: The powerful bear trend is entering its sixth week, and poised to extend below Friday's 5737.0 low. Two downwave equality targets lie close together to help form a support cluster between 5668.5 and 5679.5, but the pace of the five-week decline suggests there is scope beyond equality towards 5534.0 in the coming sessions. Corrective upside risk is limited to 5843.0, which is protected by 5817.5 and 5827.0. (published 0641 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: MORE GAINS IN STORE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Friday's gains puts the spotlight on the continuation chart high at 126.04. With good support lying in the 125.45 area, the main threat is for a sustained push higher towards 126.52 and 127.31 - the latter being the 50% Fibonacci retracement level of the 134.77/119.86 decline on the continuation chart. Only below 125.45 would create scope for a deeper corrective setback towards short-term pivotal support at 125.00. (published 0645 GMT) [ 13-06-11 1001GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110613e76d000nu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
