\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,104 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2011\par 11:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN EMPLOYERS URGE PRIVATE SECTOR PARTICIPATION IN GREECE-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The President of the German Employers' Association agrees with the government's proposal to involve private-sector bondholders in further financial help for Greece, according to an article set to be published by Hamburger Abendblatt Tuesday. (published 0902 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN'S SOCIALIST CONSIDER CALLING EARLY ELECTIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some top officials in the Spanish government, and elsewhere in the ruling Socialist party, are pushing Prime Minister Jose Luis Rodriguez Zapatero to call elections in November, well ahead of the March, 2012 deadline, El Pais reports, citing people close to the situation. (published 0815 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ITALY APRIL INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT SURGES 1.0% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian industrial output surged in April, driven by durable consumer goods, national statistics office Istat said Monday. (published 0908 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN CORE MACHINERY ORDERS POST SURPRISE DECLINE OF 3.3% IN APRIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese core machinery orders posted an unexpected 3.3% drop from a month earlier in April on the back of a decline in demand from manufacturers, the Cabinet Office said Monday. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MAY NEW YUAN LOANS DROP, MONEY SUPPLY GROWTH SLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese banks extended fewer new yuan loans in May, and money supply growth slowed, showing the impact of monetary tightening in the world's second-largest economy. (published 0338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA SCHOLAR: MUST ELIMINATE ONE-WAY BET ON YUAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China needs to eliminate expectations of a steady appreciation of the yuan and avoid a one-way bet on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that has attracted "hot money," an influential academic said. (published 0223 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OECD GURRIA: CONCERTED POLICY DECISION BY CHINA TO EASE GROWTH PACE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese authorities are making concerted efforts to cool the country's economic growth, Angel Gurria, the Secretary General of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development, said Monday. (published 0557 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: CHINA SHOULD SET UP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FUND TO CURB INFLATION \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should set up a stabilization fund to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to keep the yuan's exchange rate within a target range, a move that would curb inflation, a local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official wrote in a commentary piece on Monday. (published 0542 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA OFFICIAL: ASSET BUBBLE RISKS, INFLATION PUT PRESSURE ON HK ECONOMIC HEALTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risks of an asset bubble and rising inflation are putting pressure on the health of Hong Kong's financial system and its economy, a senior Chinese official warned Monday. (published 0525 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: SHOULD USE FISCAL RECEIPTS TO MAINTAIN STABLE YUAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should rely more on fiscal receipts rather than printing money to maintain the stability of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Zhou Qiren, an adviser to the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said in an article on his personal website Monday. (published 0517 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA INTEREST RATE HIKE NEEDED AS PRICES RISE - PBOC-BACKED NEWSPAPER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another interest rate hike in China is still needed as food and non-food prices continue to rise, a commentary piece in a newspaper backed by China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  argued Monday. (published 0026 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA WELL POISED TO SUSTAIN HIGH ECONOMIC GROWTH - FINANCE MINISTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India is poised to sustain a high rate of economic expansion for some years as the country has the growth drivers in place, despite signals that the pace of factory output growth is weakening, Finance Minister Pranab Mukherjee said Monday. (published 0523 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA FIN MIN: TO RUN MACRO POLICIES SUCH AS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , POLICY RATE 'FLEXIBLY' \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan said Monday that the authorities will continue to prioritize price stability when operating the macroeconomic policies. (published 2357 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA BANKS' SHORT-TERM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  BORROWING ROLLOVER RATE UP IN MAY VS APRIL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The proportion of short-term foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  borrowings rolled over by South Korean banks rose substantially in May from the previous month but fell short of fully replacing maturing debt, the Financial Supervisory Service said Monday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: SLUGGISH HIRING SEEN AS A THREAT TO US RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The potential for a persistent slowdown in hiring is the biggest threat to the U.S. recovery, according to economists in the latest Wall Stree