\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  should set up new fund to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on yuan -c.banker\b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juni 2011\par 04:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  BEIJING, June 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should set up a new fund to carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan stable while limiting the inflationary effect of dollar-buying, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said in remarks published on Monday.\par \par \uc2  Xu Nuojin, the deputy head of People's Bank of China Guangzhou branch, told the official Financial News that China should speed up yuan reforms to "eliminate appreciation expectations" and rein in hot money inflows.\par \par \uc2  "When there are big changes in market demand and supply that lead to big swings in the yuan exchange rate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can use the fund to buy and sell to adjust demand and supply so that the exchange rate can move in a targeted range," he said.\par \par \uc2  That will cut the link between the rise in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and yuan issuance by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the source of excessive liquidity in the economy, Xu said without elaborating.\par \par \uc2  To keep the yuan exchange rate largely stable, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  China has to buy almost all foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows from commercial banks, injecting huge amounts of yuan into the banking system.  \par \par \uc2  To counter the inflationary effect of such money inflows, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then soaks up the excess yuan in the system via open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and higher bank reserve requirements.\par \par \uc2  Xu said China invests part of its $3 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in strategic resources, including energy and minerals, to help diversity the holdings away from the dollar.\par \par \uc2  "China should use part of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy energy and important mineral resources that have strategic importance," he said.\par \par \uc2  The strategic resources could help ease the bottleneck facing China's long-term economic development, Xu added.\par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves surged to $3.05 trillion at the end of March, heightening worries about how China can effectively manage the holdings and not become overly exposed to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts estimate that at least 70 percent of the reserves is invested in U.S. government debt and other dollar assets, although the exact composition is a state secret.\par \par \uc2  Xu warned that China's inflation could become entrenched as price rises are still driven by too much cash in the economy, driven by capital inflows.\par \par \uc2  "The current inflation level is achieved through the forceful policy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government, an all-around rebound is very likely to occur once such administrative measures are relaxed," he said.\par \par \uc2   China's annual inflation is expected to have edged up to 5.4 percent in May from 5.3 percent in April and match a 32-month high seen in March.  \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Zhou Xin and Kevin Yao; Editing by Ken Wills)\par \par \uc2 CHINA-ECONOMY/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110613e76d0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2