\par\par \b \uc2 CHINA MONEY-Wider yuan trading band may not mean faster appreciation\b0\par\par\uc2 824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juni 2011\par 06:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (For recent CHINA MONEY columns, click on )\par \par \uc2  * China may be mulling widening of yuan/dlr trading band\par \par \uc2  * Only long-term impact seen if widening is announced\par \par \uc2  * Less than a quarter of existing band being used\par \par \uc2  * Forecast for yuan to rise 5 to 6 pct in 2011 unchanged \par \par \uc2  By Lu Jianxin and Jacqueline Wong\par \par \uc2  SHANGHAI, June 9 (Reuters) - Widening the yuan's daily trading band against the dollar would be a signal of Beijing's international hopes for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but investors should not expect a quicker pace of appreciation.\par \par \uc2  The yuan, also called the renminbi, has rarely tested the limit of its current band that was put in place four years ago, even though Beijing has steadily promoted the use of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  abroad and has this year even slightly accelerated\par \par \uc2 the yuan's  rise. \par \par \uc2  If China increases the 0.5 percent range the yuan  is kept in against the dollar for political reasons, as some traders speculate, the new band may only be used to its full extent over the very long term.\par \par \uc2  "China could put mechanisms for wider yuan fluctuations in place, but it can also make them sit idle for years as current practice shows," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai. \par \par \uc2  China widened the yuan's trading band to 0.5 percent on either side of a mid-point against the dollar, from 0.3 percent in May 2007. But in practice, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has confined the yuan largely to moves within 0.15 percent. On only a handful of occasions did it hit the limit.\par \par \uc2  The yuan has risen 1.8 percent versus the dollar so far this year in an apparently quicker pace of appreciation compared with 3.6 percent over all of last year.\par \par \uc2  Media and investment banks have been speculating about the possibility of a wider yuan band since May 17, when Guan Tao, a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, raised the prospect. Standard Chartered Bank, for example, expects China to double the yuan/dollar daily trading band in the next few weeks to 1 percent. [ID:nL3E7H702B] \par \par \uc2  That speculation has increased even more after Guan, head of the international payment department at the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Tuesday that market conditions for two-way movement of the yuan's exchange rate have now ripened.\par \par \uc2  He later said his comments, which were published in articles posted on official websites, had been made in private academic discussions and only represented his personal views.[ID:nL3E7H71AA]\par \par \uc2  A WANDERING TIGER\par \par \uc2  For now traders in China believe that where there is smoke, there is fire, or perhaps a tiger.\par \par \uc2  "The market is now buzzing with talk of yuan trading band widening, but few appear to have got a confirmation from officials senior enough to make the decision," said a senior trader at a Chinese state-owned bank in Beijing.\par \par \uc2  "There is a Chinese saying that if you have heard three people talking of a tiger wandering in a town, you believe it."\par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, dealers have kept epenciled in that the yuan would rise 5 to 6 percent for all 2011, and have shrugged off any short-term impact of a wider trading band -- a step that would be taken for presumably political ends.\par \par \uc2   In reality, if China widens the yuan's trading band, it may invite more speculative capital inflows to bet on yuan rises in the short term, enhancing the need for the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  (PBOC) \uc2 to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading so as to keep the pace of yuan appreciation under tighter control, traders said. \par \par \uc2  One theory among traders is that a wider band was part of a deal struck at the U.S.-China Strategic and Economic Dialogue in Washington in early May, just before Guan called for a change. China may just be waiting for the right time to announce it.\par \par \uc2  The Chinese government seeks to make the yuan part of the International Monetary Fund's Special Drawing Right (SDR) regime by 2015, among other efforts to boost the profile of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its global economic influence. [ID:nL3E7G5008]\par \par \uc2  This year, the  PBOC, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 has guided the yuan to a slew of record highs on the dollar as part of its campaign to fight inflation. The market has seen slightly greater yuan volatility since it was depegged from the dollar in June 2010.\par \par \uc2  The authorities have so far kept to a gradual pace of yuan appreciation even though greater volatility is to be expected as China strives to develop and deepen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and takes bolder steps toward making the yuan fully convertible.\par \par \uc2  With international calls for the yuan to rise in the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  arena becoming more urgent, bigger daily swings may start to happen, but it will still take time, traders said. \par \par \uc2 (Editing by Kevin Plumberg)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-CHINA/DEBT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110612e7690002q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
