\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ TIMELINE for yuan report\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jean Yung   \par \uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2011\par 07:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 May 25, 1993\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury labels China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator in a report to Congress. China is kept on the list in September 1993 and July 1994.\par \par \uc2 Jan. 1, 1994\par \par \uc2 China unifies its dual exchange-rate system by moving the official rate of 5.8 to the dollar to the prevailing market-determined rate of 8.7, devaluing the yuan by 33% overnight as part of reforms to establish a socialist market economy. The new "controlled floating-rate system" lets the yuan move on the market within a narrow government-determined range.\par \par \uc2 Dec. 1, 1996\par \par \uc2 China allows the yuan to be fully convertible into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for trade purposes but maintains rules and limits on buying and selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make loans and investments.\par \par \uc2 1997-99\par \par \uc2 China wins praise for sticking to its commitment not to devalue the yuan during the Asian financial crisis and helping to stabilize the region. The yuan was pegged at 8.28 to the dollar through frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2001-05\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins to relax its capital controls gradually after joining the World Trade Organization, but international pressure mounts for China to let the yuan appreciate faster to help balance global trade.\par \par \uc2 February 2004\par \par \uc2 China allows Hong Kong banks to offer limited retail yuan banking services to facilitate cross-border tourist spending.\par \par \uc2 July 21, 2005\par \par \uc2 China shifts from a decade-old peg against the dollar to a managed float. based on a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It revalues the yuan by 2.1% overnight to 8.11 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 July 2007\par \par \uc2 First yuan-denominated bonds are sold in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 July 2008-June 2010\par \par \uc2 China effectively pegs the yuan against the dollar at 6.83 as an emergency measure to help stabilize China's economy amid the worsening global financial and economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 July 2009\par \par \uc2 China launches trial program allowing companies in select cities to settle imports and exports in yuan, naming Hong Kong the pilot city for yuan settlement outside of the mainland.\par \par \uc2 June 19, 2010\par \par \uc2 China pledges to increase the yuan's trading flexibility and effectively ends a two-year-long peg to the dollar, returning to a managed float based on a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 July 19, 2010\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  agree to expand the scope of yuan clearing in Hong Kong, and offshore yuan trading begins to take off.\par \par \uc2 Aug. 20, 2010\par \par \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  becomes first non-financial foreign company to issue a yuan bonds Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Oct 1, 2010\par \par \uc2 China allows select exporters to keep some of their foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earnings offshore in a trial program.\par \par \uc2 Jan. 12, 2011\par \par \uc2 Bank of China opens yuan trading for U.S. customers.\par \par \uc2 Jan. 13, 2011\par \par \uc2 China launches a pilot program to let domestic companies use yuan for investments outside China.\par \par \uc2 Feb. 4, 2011\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury says China's exchange rate is "substantially undervalued" but declines to name China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 March-April 2011\par \par \uc2 About 7% of China's foreign trade is settled in yuan in the first quarter, most of it with Hong Kong. By the end of April, yuan deposits in Hong Kong climb to 511 billion yuan ($79 billion), or 8.4% of total deposits.\par \par \uc2 April 28, 2011\par \par \uc2 Li Ka-Shing's \uc2 Hui Xian Real Estate Investment Trust\uc2  becomes the first yuan-denominated initial public offering outside of China but falls sharply on its debut on the Hong Kong exchange.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110602e762003ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
