\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Hits Fresh Record High Late; Traders See More Gains Ahead\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2011\par 10:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity  Previous \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.4845               6.4856 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.4791    -0.08%     6.4829 \par              High        6.4831    -0.02% \par              Low         6.4780    -0.10% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan rose to a fresh record high against the U.S. dollar late Tuesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guided the yuan higher using its reference exchange rate in response to a globally weak dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The yuan broke a psychological milestone by sailing past 6.4800, which traders said signalled that China will allow the yuan to gain steadily in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.4791 around 0830 GMT, down from CNY6.4829 late Monday. It traded between CNY6.4780, its lowest level since China's landmark \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reforms in 2005, and CNY6.4831.\par \par \uc2 At CNY6.4780 to the dollar, the yuan has risen 5.4% against the U.S. unit since June, when China effectively ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two-year-long peg to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The yuan gained 0.2% against the dollar in May, slowing from a 0.9% gain in April.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  fixed the dollar/yuan central parity rate at a fresh record low for the fourth straight session after the dollar fell to a three-week low against the euro. The reference rate was set at 6.4845, down from Monday's 6.4856.\par \par \uc2 Some dealers said they thought the PBOC may support the dollar at around 6.4800 to stall the yuan's gains, but "the PBOC allowed the pair to fall through 6.4800, meaning we can expect more gradual gains to come," said a Shanghai-based trader at an Asian bank.\par \par \uc2 At 0830 GMT, the euro was at $1.4400 from $1.4282 in late trading in Toronto on Monday as concerns over Greece's debt crisis eased. U.S. markets were closed for Memorial Day.\par \par \uc2 A former Chinese regulator said Monday that if \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by purchasing the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  that enters the country, the yuan would likely rise to CNY4 or CNY5 against the dollar, meaning an appreciation of up to 62% from the current level.\par \par \uc2 Chen Bingcai, the former vice head of the Capital Account Management Department of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was speaking at a forum on the management of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards were down at 6.3550/6.3600 from 6.3580/6.3630 late Monday, implying a 2.0% rise by the yuan against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year.\par \par \uc2 -By Jean Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; jean.yung@dowjones.com [ 31-05-11 0857GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110531e75v000r0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
