\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Steady Late On  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Guidance After U.S. Talks End\b0\par\par\uc2 795 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2011\par 11:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity    Pvs \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.4948              6.4950 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.4930    -0.03%    6.4919 \par              High        6.4942    -0.01% \par              Low         6.4905    -0.07% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan was almost unchanged against the U.S. dollar late Wednesday as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  guided its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  steady via a reference exchange rate after the conclusion of high-level talks between China and the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.4930 around 0830 GMT, up a tad from CNY6.4919 late Tuesday. It traded between CNY6.4905 and CNY6.4942.\par \par \uc2 At CNY6.4930 to the dollar, the yuan has risen 5.1% against the U.S. unit since June, when China effectively ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two-year-long peg to the dollar and vowed to make the yuan more flexible.\par \par \uc2 Before the trading began, the People's Bank of China fixed the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.4948, down marginally from Tuesday's 6.4950 and marking the third consecutive record low.\par \par \uc2 "The steady central parity has set a tone for the spot market today: The PBOC intends to let the yuan consolidate for now," said a Shanghai-based trader at a European bank.\par \par \uc2 Zhu Guangyao, China's vice minister of the finance department, reiterated Tuesday that while China and the U.S. agree on the direction of yuan exchange-rate reform, there is disagreement over the pace.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials have said they have seen a marked change in tone in negotiations over China's carefully managed exchange-rate policy as Washington seeks to prod Beijing to pick up the pace of yuan appreciation. Inflation pressures are building, prompting a raft of senior Chinese analysts and economists to call for a more radical appreciation of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So far, China hasn't strayed much from an informal pledge it made to the U.S. late last year to boost the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by about 0.5% a month, which is equivalent to around a 6.0% rise in the yuan for the full year, a market consensus for the yuan.\par \par \uc2 The dollar briefly neared a psychologically significant support at CNY6.4900 during the session, but likely \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported the U.S. unit, two traders said.\par \par \uc2 "We suspect that the PBOC bought dollars on the anonymous automatic price-matching above CNY6.4900 today to stall the yuan's rise, a likely indication the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intends to let the CNY consolidate for at least a few more sessions," said a Shanghai-based trader at a local bank. The price-matching system is a little-used anonymous trading platform that dealers believe the PBOC uses on occasion to signal its intentions to the market, while not officially showing its hand.\par \par \uc2 However, further gains in the yuan is still in the cards, dealers said, citing higher-than-expected inflation rate data for April indicates China may launch further monetary tightening measures, including a stronger yuan.\par \par \uc2 China's consumer price index rose 5.3% in April from a year earlier, slower than the 5.4% rise in March, but higher than the median forecast of a 5.2% gain in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 14 economists.\par \par \uc2 "The inflation data may suggest that Beijing could tighten its monetary policy further, or at least won't ease its tightening efforts for now," said a Shanghai-based trader at an Asian bank, adding that a stronger yuan is an optional policy tool to cool inflation.\par \par \uc2 Another Shanghai-based Asian bank trader said, "We expect the yuan to rise steadily this month as pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is both internal--high inflation--and external--from the U.S."\par \par \uc2 A lack of overt U.S. pressure during its strategic and economic dialogue with China may make it easier for China to extend a string of record low USD/CNY fixings, which in turn makes it easier for the U.S. Treasury to issue its bi-annual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report without criticising the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, Credit Agricole wrote in a research note. The house added that "there was nothing new regarding the outlook for CNY appreciation. Both sides agreed to disagree on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on other major economic issues as the bilateral meeting ended earlier."\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards fell to 6.3340/6.3390 from 6.3450/6.3500 late Tuesday, implying a 2.5% rise by the yuan against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year.\par \par \uc2 -By Amy Li, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; amy.li@dowjones.com [ 11-05-11 0937GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110511e75b0011p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
