\par\par \b \uc2 DJ 2nd UPDATE: PBOC Plans  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 -Reserve Investment Funds -Report\b0\par\par\uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2011\par 05:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to add analysis of resource investment in reform in fifth paragraph, analysis of reform to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system in nineth and tenth paragraphs, comment from PBOC researcher in the 12th paragraph)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is planning to set up a variety of new funds that will make use of the country's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, including special-purpose investment funds and a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization fund dedicated to \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a local news website reported Monday, citing unnamed sources.\par \par \uc2 'Relevant authorities' are studying a proposal by the People's Bank of China to create funds using some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets from the PBOC's balance sheet that could invest in sectors such as energy and precious metals, as well as a fund that will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to influence \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, Caixin Media said, citing a source close to the PBOC.\par \par \uc2 The source said the funds would be loosely modeled on the Norwegian sovereign wealth fund commonly referred to as the Petroleum Fund, which uses the country's oil profits to insulate the economy from price fluctuations, the report said.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are the largest in the world, totaling $3.0447 trillion at the end of March, up from $2.8473 trillion at the end of 2010, according to the PBOC.\par \par \uc2 Various academics and policymakers in China have frequently proposed that the country direct more of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves into resources and commodities, but others, such as People's Bank of China Vice Gov. Yi Gang, have countered that it would be difficult to do so without significantly increasing the market prices of those goods.\par \par \uc2 The proposed \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization fund would allow the PBOC to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly on the market without having to print new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  notes, the report said, citing people familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC already regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by issuing yuan in exchange for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which prevents the yuan from rising too quickly but also adds to inflationary pressures by increasing liquidity.\par \par \uc2 If Beijing capitalizes the proposed fund by a special bond sale, as it did when it set up \uc2 China Investment Corp\uc2 ., the country's sovereign wealth fund, it could avoid adding to the money supply with new yuan issues.\par \par \uc2 Currently, Beijing manages the exchange rate daily by setting a central-parity rate around which it is narrowly allowed to trade. Shifting to the use of a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund to step into the market only occasionally would suggest a substantial reform to the exchange rate regime, one that is likely a far way off.\par \par \uc2 The report provided no further details as to how the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund would operate, except to site previous studies by an academic at a state think-tank and a local PBOC official, who both broadly outlined a looser \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime whereby Beijing would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the yuan within a defined range.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  declined to immediately comment on the report. Zhang Jianhua, head of the PBOC research bureau, said he is not familiar with the proposals.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  financed CIC's launch in 2007 by effectively transferring part of a huge special bond issue to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange for some of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings, and selling the rest on the interbank market.\par \par \uc2 PBOC Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan said last week the government is working to reduce the accumulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which pump excess cash into the economy and 'exceed our reasonable requirements.'\par \par \uc2 'There is no comprehensive or systematic solution, but the PBOC has to think of ways to stabilize the domestic money supply, otherwise it will lead to asset bubbles. Such a fund would be an attempt at finding a way,' Chen Xingdong, economist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilization fund is generally composed of gold, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the report said.\par \par \uc2 Word of the possible stabilization fund comes after Xu Nuojin, vice governor of PBOC's Guangzhou branch, said in an article earlier this year that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could keep fluctuations in the yuan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate within a targeted range by selling and buying \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  via such a fund.\par \par \uc2 -Jean Yung, Aaron Back, and Stefanie Qi contributed to this article; Dow Jones Newswires; (8610) 8400-7331; jean.yung@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110427e74q001p6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
