\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Yuan Falls By Daily Limit Against Dollar; PBOC Likely  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2011\par 11:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan fell by its daily limit against the U.S. dollar late Monday after banks scrambled to buy dollars to meet a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rule barring them from having dollar short positions overnight, after several large import settlements created a short squeeze in the last few minutes of trading.\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar hit an intraday high of CNY6.5487 shortly before 0830 GMT, up by the 0.5% daily limit from Monday's dollar/yuan central parity of 6.5161.\par \par \uc2 The central parity is the midpoint set by the People's Bank of China, around which the yuan can rise or fall by 0.5% every day.\par \par \uc2 Traders said a likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the PBOC shortly after the yuan hit its lower limit allowed the dollar to pull back to CNY6.5277 around 0830 GMT.\par \par \uc2 The turnaround came after someone posted an offer to sell dollars on the automatic price-matching system, a little-used anonymous trading platform that dealers believe the PBOC uses on occasion to signal its intentions to the market, while not officially showing its hand.\par \par \uc2 One or more transactions were recorded at CNY6.5250, well within the 0.5% lower limit, around 0830 GMT on the price-matching system, according to the PBOC-supervised China \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trade System, which runs China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 "After the yuan hit its daily limit, still more banks were looking to buy dollars, so the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to provide liquidity in the market," a trader at a foreign bank in Shenzhen said.\par \par \uc2 -By Jean Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; jean.yung@dowjones.com [ 25-04-11 0923GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110425e74p000fj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
