\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Nations Seen Taking More Subdued Tone On  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Wars\b0\par\par\uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2011\par 21:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Emerging nations may be less strident on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation at coming IMF/World Bank meetings\par \par \uc2 --Inflation makes emerging countries more open to \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation\par \par \uc2 --Capital controls, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles to remain an issue\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Anjali Cordeiro \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--After a year of aggressive moves to beat down their exchange rates, emerging nations are quietly showing a greater acceptance for stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as they battle rising inflation at home.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some of that willingness to allow \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation may be reflected in the tone of finance ministers and central bankers from the developing world as they gather for the 2011 Spring meetings of the World Bank and International Monetary Fund in Washington later this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As with previous such meetings, there is still likely to be finger pointing. Developing nations continue to blame loose monetary policy in the U.S. as a cause of potential asset bubbles and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation in Asia and Latin America. They also face pressure from their exporters to keep \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weak due to competition from China, which tightly controls the yuan. But those worries are temporarily taking a back seat to local price pressures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I wouldn't expect the same stridency around \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that we have seen in some recent meetings, nor will the issue disappear," says Mark Malloch-Brown, a former vice president of the World Bank, who is now at FTI Consulting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For investors, the growing possibility that policymakers will be more flexible makes Latin American and Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  an attractive proposition. The dollar has lost 5.6% against the real and 1.4% against the Indonesia rupiah since mid March. A stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which reduces the price of imports, can help reduce inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega, who used the term "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wars" to describe global disagreements on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , last year raised taxes on foreign fixed-income investments into the country. But as the real soared over the last month, investors grew hopeful that Brazilian policymakers will take a softer stance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has continued to build reserves, there have been no stringent capital controls announced. Policymakers there are battling inflation, with data showing a rise in the 12-month inflation rate to 6.3% as of March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia's deputy governor said recently that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to fight inflation and prevent the rupiah from being overly volatile. Those comments could suggest the bank might be open to allowing the rupiah to rise somewhat to fight inflation, but it would want any moves to be smooth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Indonesia changed their tune," said David Rolley, portfolio manager at Loomis, Sayles & Co. "All these central banks had to decide what war to fight. They are now much more interested in fighting inflation than a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One issue likely to be hotly debated at the meetings is the efficacy of capital controls and government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In a reversal of its earlier stance, the International Monetary Fund recently endorsed the use of controls on international cash inflows in certain cases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it is still unclear how effective such policy tools have been in curbing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increases in places like Brazil and South Korea. These \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have risen even though policymakers have taken steps to curb so-called "hot money" flows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging nations are unlikely to completely throw in the towel. Central banks in South Korea, Malaysia and Indonesia appeared to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in recent weeks as their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  jumped, though their main focus appears to be to stem appreciation rather than reverse the direction of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging Asian countries, excluding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by an estimated $11 billion in March and $15.3 billion in February, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . This compares to an average of $22.6 billion per month between August and October last year, when such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were more aggressive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging-market policymakers generally compare their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  versus others in the developing world. So, they tend to be more comfortable if all emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are appreciating at the same time, says Denise Simon, a portfolio manager on the Lazard emerging-markets debt team in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't know if individually countries can do anything" to reverse the upward trend in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Simon, whose team likes Asian and Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because of the fundamental strength of these economies and widening interest rate differentials.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging world central banks have begun raising interest rates, even as rates in the U.S. and Japan remain near zero. The International Monetary Fund in its World Economic Outlook noted that some emerging economies will need even tighter monetary policies to reduce the risk of a hard landing. In most of these economies, some appreciation of the exchange rate is called for, the IMF said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philip Poole, head of macro and investments strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Global Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says China's recent moves to let the yuan to move a little higher makes it easier for other emerging nations to allow appreciation. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the Singapore dollar and Korean won look attractive, he feels, and even the Brazilian real could rise some more.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he doesn't expect any broad consensus or coordination on emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the IMF meetings: "It's each man for himself."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anjali Cordeiro; Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2200; anjali.cordeiro@dowjones.com [ 04-12-11 1549ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110412e74c000ny\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
