\par\par \b \uc2 Former PBOC Adviser: US Treasury Bond Market Bolstered By 'Ponzi Scheme'\b0\par\par\uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2011\par 11:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--A prominent Chinese economist and former adviser to the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday likened the market for U.S. Treasury bonds to a giant ponzi scheme, and argued China should float the yuan in part so it doesn't have to acquire so many Treasury assets.\par \par \uc2 "China should have retreated from the U.S. government bond market a long time ago," Yu Yongding, currently an economist at a state think tank and formerly a member of the People's Bank of China monetary policy committee, wrote in an essay on the Caixin Media Group website.\par \par \uc2 "The (U.S government) bond market was essentially bolstered by a ponzi scheme. The U.S. Federal Reserve's policy of quantitative easing has artificially kept the bonds at a high price. However, the market price of the bonds will eventually fall to a level determined by the fundamentals of the U.S economy," Yu added.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should allow a free floating yuan, he argued, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market only when necessary, as this would reduce the need to acquire U.S. dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 An appreciation of the yuan would also help control money supply growth and inflation, he added.\par \par \uc2 Yu has no official sway over policy, and his views could hardly be considered the consensus among policymakers. But his sharply worded essay is a window into the contentious debate, taking place largely behind closed doors, between various top officials and scholars in China on the country's exchange rate and foreign investment policies.\par \par \uc2 When foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  enters China through exports or foreign investment, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys up the bulk of that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with yuan to prevent the value of the yuan from rising. The added yuan issuance swells the domestic money supply, adding to inflation pressures, but the PBOC attempts to offset this effect by issuing its own bills and through other so-called "sterilization" measures.\par \par \uc2 The extent to which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is successful at offsetting this money creation is widely debated. Yu argued in his essay that it is becoming more difficult to issue the liquidity-absorbing bills, which come at a cost the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it must pay interest to bill holders, and that the accumulation of foreign reserves has been the most important driver behind China's money supply.\par \par \uc2 "The economic and welfare costs from China's too-slow move in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment is indeed too high, and will continue to surge," he said. China must choose between "either endure the short-term pain of yuan appreciation, or face a cruel reality that in the future, its huge amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves may not be able to buy anything," Yu said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. dollar depreciation is causing capital losses on China's stock of U.S. Treasury bonds, Yu said. The purchasing power of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves has lagged far behind their nominal increase when measured against commodities such as gold or oil, he said.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the world's biggest, totalled $2.8473 trillion at the end of 2010, up 18.7% from a year earlier, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. Most of the reserves were invested in U.S. dollar assets.\par \par \uc2 China held $1.11 trillion of U.S. Treasury debt as of June 30, 2010, according to data issued by the U.S. Treasury on Feb. 28. The data was revised upward from earlier estimates, but analysts say it still may underestimate slightly the size of China's holdings.\par \par \uc2 "It's already too late for China to reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve holdings sharply without incurring severe political and financial problems," he said, "but at least China should stop further increase to its total \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves."\par \par \uc2 -Eliot Gao and Aaron Back contributed to this article, Dow Jones Newswires; (86 10) 8400-7705; eliot.gao@dowjones.com [ 11-04-11 0919GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110411e74b000qr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
