\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,107 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPE DEFAULT INSURANCE COSTS EDGE LOWER, IRISH BANKS EYED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost of insuring European corporate and sovereign debt against default edged lower in early trading Thursday, after U.S. stocks rose and ahead of the announcement of Irish bank stress test results. (published 0746 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH FEB PPI +0.8% ON MONTH; +6.3% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French producer prices rose substantially in February, as refining and coking prices rose by a third from last year, according to data released Thursday by the French national statistics office Insee. (published 0747 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: US GEITHNER ARGUES FOR FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Flexible exchange rates can help countries better absorb shocks and better tailor monetary policies to their individual circumstances, Geithner said in prepared remarks that he is to deliver at the Nanjing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seminar. (published 0244 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SARKOZY URGES GLOBAL RETHINK OF LIQUIDITY PROVISION RULES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French president Nicolas Sarkozy Thursday urged the group of 20 industrialized and emerging nations to seek ways to better manage global liquidity especially in times of crisis, including possibly allowing the International Monetary Fund to tap financial markets to increase its lending capacity. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA VICE PREMIER WANG SHUNS YUAN TOPIC AT KEY G-20 GATHERING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Vice Premier Wang Qishan said Thursday that Beijing will work with others toward "a more equitable global monetary system" but avoided any reference to the sensitive topic of the yuan's exchange rate at a closely watched meeting of the Group of 20 industrial and developing countries. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC ZHOU: GLOBAL ECONOMIC ADJUSTMENT SHOULD FOCUS ON LONG TERM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The international community should focus on long-term reform of the international monetary system and not quick fixes, said People's Bank of China Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan Thursday in comments suggesting U.S. insistence that China allow the yuan to appreciate more quickly is misguided. (published 0507 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: NATIONAL BANK OF NEW ZEALAND MARCH BUSINESS CONFIDENCE -9.0%; FEB +34.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business confidence in New Zealand tumbled in March from February as sentiment was hurt by a 6.3-magnitude earthquake that devastated New Zealand's second largest city of Christchurch in late February, a leading survey showed Thursday. (published 0317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par HUNGARY 4Q CURRENT ACCOUNT SURPLUS HIGHER THAN SEEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's current account generated a higher-than-expected surplus in the fourth quarter of last year, contributing to the country's highest ever annual current account surplus in 2010, data released Thursday by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  show. (published 0735 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SARKOZY URGES GLOBAL RETHINK OF LIQUIDITY PROVISION RULES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French President Nicolas Sarkozy Thursday urged the Group of 20 industrialized and emerging nations to seek ways to better manage global liquidity especially in times of crisis, including possibly allowing the International Monetary Fund to tap financial markets to increase its lending capacity. (published 0720 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC ADVISER LI: INTERNATIONAL MONETARY SYSTEM REFORM NEEDS TO BE GRADUAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A prominent Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser said Thursday reform of the international monetary system should be carried out gradually so as to minimize the negative impact on China's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN DOESN'T PLAN TO WIDEN NUCLEAR EVACUATION ZONE DESPITE IAEA FINDINGS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan has no immediate plan to expand the evacuation zone around the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant despite a finding showing higher-than-permitted levels of radiation outside the current zone and a continued rise in radiation levels in nearby ocean water, the government said Thursday. (published 0559 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK CONSUMER CONFIDENCE REMAINS STEADY IN MARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumer confidence remained steady in March as Britons became more upbeat about the outlook for the economy, but more worried about their personal finances. (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN FINANCE MINISTER: PORTUGAL HAS LIMITED IMPACT ON SPAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trust by financial markets globally in the euro zone has increased in recent months as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area has moved to find solutions for its problems, German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said Wednesday. (published 0022 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIA CONSUMERS SHRUG OFF FLOODS, RETURN TO SHOP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian consumers in February shrugged off the effects of some of the nation's worst natural disasters and returned to the high street, boosting retail sales beyond economists expectations. (published 0453 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIA CENTRAL BANKS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS US, FRANCE PRESS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ON YUAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea and Malaysia were seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday to slow the rise of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the yuan at a record high and the U.S. and France called for more flexibility in exchange rates--the latest sign they want Beijing to let its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rise faster. (published 0514 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par POST-JAPAN QUAKE SURVEY SHOWS SHARP DROP IN BUSINESS CONDITIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the devastating earthquake that struck Japan earlier this month, manufacturing activity deteriorated at its fastest pace on record, a survey by a private research firm showed Thursday. (published 2332 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  ====== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/USD SET FOR PUSH HIGHER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The euro is poised to win this intriguing two-day standoff, and a push above 1.4149 is expected to open projected resistance at 1.4188. A push through 1.4188 is required to keep the uptrend intact, opening 1.4220 and the Mar. 22 reaction high at 1.4249. The 1.4095 area will look to contain weakness, but only a break below Wednesday's 1.4052 low would concern bulls. (published 0712 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EUROPEAN EQUITY INDEX TECHNICALS: BULLS STILL IN CHARGE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday FTSE 100: Bulls retain control of the short-term, and resistance at 5940.0 is likely to be broken. The wave equality target at 5994.0 is within reach, which lies just beneath the Mar. 4 lower high at 5996.0, and together with the psychological importance of the 6000.0 level, this area will look to protect the Feb. 21 contract high at 6042.0. Solid support at 5875.0 will look to cushion corrective weakness, but only below 5832.0 would concern bulls. (published 0713 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: CORRECTING HIGHER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: The recovery off 121.16 is likely to struggle, and has scope to the 121.80 area before bears look to re-emerge. The sharpness of the setback off the 122.74 high highlights the dominant bear tone, and the main threat is for a downtrend extension below 121.16 towards the Feb. 9 contract low at 120.92. A downwave equality target lies at 120.61. A break above 121.80 would provide respite, but projected resistance at 122.11 is not expected to yield. (published 0752 GMT) [ 31-03-11 0659GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110331e73v000ib\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
