\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 907 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: S KOREA OUTPUT CONTRACTS IN FEBRUARY; FIRST TIME IN 4 MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's factory output contracted for the first time in four months in February as widely expected, due mainly to fewer working days because of the Lunar New Year holidays. (published 0110 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIAN DOLLAR POSTS NEW HIGH ON SOLID RETAIL SALES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian retail sales were stronger than expected in February bolstered by a rise in discretionary spending as consumers returned to stores targeting clothing and goods to furnish homes. (published 0131 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIA CONSUMERS SHRUG OFF FLOODS, RETURN TO SHOP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian consumers in February shrugged off the effects of some of the nation's worst natural disasters and returned to the high street, boosting retail sales beyond economists expectations. (published 0453 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIA CENTRAL BANKS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS US, FRANCE PRESS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ON YUAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea and Malaysia were seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday to slow the rise of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the yuan at a record high and the U.S. and France called for more flexibility in exchange rates--the latest sign they want Beijing to let its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rise faster. (published 0514 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par POST-JAPAN QUAKE SURVEY SHOWS SHARP DROP IN BUSINESS CONDITIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the devastating earthquake that struck Japan earlier this month, manufacturing activity deteriorated at its fastest pace on record, a survey by a private research firm showed Thursday. (published 2332 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: US GEITHNER ARGUES FOR FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Flexible exchange rates can help countries better absorb shocks and better tailor monetary policies to their individual circumstances, Geithner said in prepared remarks that he is to deliver at the Nanjing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seminar. (published 0244 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SARKOZY URGES GLOBAL RETHINK OF LIQUIDITY PROVISION RULES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French president Nicolas Sarkozy Thursday urged the group of 20 industrialized and emerging nations to seek ways to better manage global liquidity especially in times of crisis, including possibly allowing the International Monetary Fund to tap financial markets to increase its lending capacity. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA VICE PREMIER WANG SHUNS YUAN TOPIC AT KEY G-20 GATHERING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Vice Premier Wang Qishan said Thursday that Beijing will work with others toward "a more equitable global monetary system" but avoided any reference to the sensitive topic of the yuan's exchange rate at a closely watched meeting of the Group of 20 industrial and developing countries. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC ZHOU: GLOBAL ECONOMIC ADJUSTMENT SHOULD FOCUS ON LONG TERM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The international community should focus on long-term reform of the international monetary system and not quick fixes, said People's Bank of China Gov. Zhou Xiaochuan Thursday in comments suggesting U.S. insistence that China allow the yuan to appreciate more quickly is misguided. (published 0507 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: NATIONAL BANK OF NEW ZEALAND MARCH BUSINESS CONFIDENCE -9.0%; FEB +34.5% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business confidence in New Zealand tumbled in March from February as sentiment was hurt by a 6.3-magnitude earthquake that devastated New Zealand's second largest city of Christchurch in late February, a leading survey showed Thursday. (published 0317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK OF KOREA: 2011 INFLATION TO STAY WAY ABOVE MIDPOINT OF 2%-4% TARGET BAND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea Thursday said it expects inflation this year to stay way above the 3% midpoint of its mid-term target band and vowed to run monetary policy to keep prices stable. (published 0500 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA PBOC ADVISER: SHOULD DEVELOP ASIAN DOLLAR BOND MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China adviser Xia Bin called Thursday for efforts to develop the Asian dollar-denominated bond market. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC ADVISER LI: INTERNATIONAL MONETARY SYSTEM REFORM NEEDS TO BE GRADUAL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A prominent Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  adviser said Thursday reform of the international monetary system should be carried out gradually so as to minimize the negative impact on China's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves. (published 0155 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN DOESN'T PLAN TO WIDEN NUCLEAR EVACUATION ZONE DESPITE IAEA FINDINGS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan has no immediate plan to expand the evacuation zone around the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant despite a finding showing higher-than-permitted levels of radiation outside the current zone and a continued rise in radiation levels in nearby ocean water, the government said Thursday. (published 0559 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: UK CONSUMER CONFIDENCE REMAINS STEADY IN MARCH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumer confidence remained steady in March as Britons became more upbeat about the outlook for the economy, but more worried about their personal finances. (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN FINANCE MINISTER: PORTUGAL HAS LIMITED IMPACT ON SPAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trust by financial markets globally in the euro zone has increased in recent months as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area has moved to find solutions for its problems, German Finance Minister Wolfgang Schaeuble said Wednesday. (published 0022 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA MARCH ANNUAL INFLATION LIKELY 6.94% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's on-year inflation is expected to edge higher in March due to a low base of comparison, though on a monthly basis the country will likely report deflation. (published 0305 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: HANDICAPPING THE ECONOMIC RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If you're searching for a metaphor for the U.S. economy right now, think of an athlete who is recovering from serious injuries and must navigate a difficult obstacle course. She's getting into better shape but there are hazards along the way that might keep her from reaching the finish line. (published 2321 GMT) [ 31-03-11 0601GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110331e73v000dd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
