\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ/The Source: Dollar Behaving Strangely? Blame China\b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 14:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This article has been posted on The Source, the Wall Street Journal Online's site for European real-time analysis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/source"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/source}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted by Eva Szalay \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 People love conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 One in particular has been doing the rounds in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  land for months, with no sign of fizzling out. Right now, it's having a mini revival.\par \par \uc2 The theory goes as follows:\par \par \uc2 China is buying euros in a deliberate effort to stop the yuan from rising too far. Sure, it's keeping Washington sweet by letting the yuan climb against the dollar. But it's bashing the buck hard by buying euros, keeping the yuan relatively weak on a trade-weighted basis.\par \par \uc2 The analysts at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  are keen on this idea right now.\par \par \uc2 "China fixes [the dollar] lower [against the yuan] trying to hold increasing commodity import price pressure at the border," the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said Monday in a note to clients. Translation: commodities prices are rising, so China is letting the yuan climb, to make those imports cheaper.\par \par \uc2 But, the bank continues: "China seems to be not yet ready accepting general yuan strength, suggesting that the People's Bank of China reserve managers will remain on the euro bid." Again, a translation for those uninitiated in the dark art of reading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  research: China still wants to keep the yuan generally weak, so it will keep on buying euros.\par \par \uc2 Now that the grand G-7 coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the yen has taken place, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't very easily buy Japanese yen to help keep the yuan under pressure, so this euro buying is likely to accelerate, the French bank added.\par \par \uc2 This may or may not be true. It's close to impossible to tell for sure, although it chimes with persistent trading-floor chatter. Whenever the euro runs higher against the buck, with no obvious trigger, traders blame China. Many market watchers are nonplussed by this idea, pointing out that there's a good number of other reasons for the euro to be strong against the dollar right now, despite the long-running European debt crisis. After all, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates are set to rise within three weeks. We'll be lucky if the Fed does the same this year. To be fair, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  doesn't think this is the only reason for euro strength, either.\par \par \uc2 But if it is true, it goes some way towards solving one of the biggest riddles in the market: the curious case of the weak dollar. With investors' worry lists running into at least two pages, it's weird that the dollar--usually a safe retreat in times of stress--is not significantly stronger.\par \par \uc2 Pinning this on China may be misguided. But the euro's resilience is somewhat odd. And two plus two makes at least five in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 . [ 22-03-11 1331GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110322e73m0013u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
