\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Events Conspire To Push USD Lower Overnight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 09:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0801 GMT [Dow Jones] The greenback slipped overnight, with the USD Index trading down at levels last seen in November of 2009. EUR/USD garnered support from news EU officials have agreed on details of how the ESM facility can be used, GBP/USD is bid ahead of what is expected to be a high inflation print, while USD/JPY is a tad lower as exporter sellers re-enter the market after Japan's three day weekend. Positive closes for both the DJIA and the Nikkei, +1.5% and +4.4% respectively, have also given the risk \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a lift, to the detriment off the USD and CHF. For Tuesday, the already-mentioned UK CPI is the day's data highlight, while MENA unrest and events in Japan will also vie for attention. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0754 GMT [Dow Jones] June bunds are unchanged at 122.17 and WestLB sees support for the contract at 121.71, ahead of 121.42, and 121.12. Resistance is at 122.74, 123.21, and 123.92. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] The Polish zloty is now very vulnerable to a decline in portfolio inflows as the surplus of Poland's balance of payments has recently started to evaporate, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Poland still has relatively ample reserves and access to EUR22 billion from the IMF, but the country's used up its balance of payments buffer, BarCap adds. EUR/PLN is at 4.0259 from 4.0237 late Monday. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi ends up 0.5% at 2013.66 as foreigners lapped up local shares for a fourth straight day amid easing concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis, analysts say. Chemical and oil-related stocks led the day's gains on expectations those companies will benefit from anticipated production losses for their Japanese counterparts following the massive earthquake. "Until we start hearing about production normalization for Japanese companies, investor sentiment is likely to be centered around chemical and oil refining related stocks," in Korea, says Taurus Investment & Securities analyst Lee Kyung-su, who tips the index to test its 60-day moving average around 2031 in the near term. \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) ended up 4.2% at KRW222,500, S-Oil (010950.SE) ended 2.5% higher at KRW144,000, while \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) rose 2.2% to KRW424,000 and \uc2 Honam Petrochemical\uc2  (011170.SE) gained 5.3% to KRW400,000. Tech shares underperformed the broader market on persisting concerns about their 1Q earnings; \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) fell 1.7% to KRW875,000 and \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) slipped 0.5% to KRW101,500. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end up amid increasing expectations that the PBOC has no plans to raise interest rates immediately, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves the yield on the one-year bills sold in its regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  unchanged from the week before, says a Beijing-based trader with a local insurer. "It seems the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prefers quantitative measures, such as selling bills in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and raising reserve requirement ratios, rather than interest rate hikes in containing inflation at the current stage," he says. The Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 127.54 compared with Monday's close at 127.53, and is likely to extend gains near-term as liquidity in the money market remains in excess despite the latest RRR hike. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0737 GMT [Dow Jones] China is likely to reduce its daily crude runs substantially in March from record levels last month due to maintenance and relatively high domestic fuel stocks. Refineries are also operating on thin margins, as the government waits to raise prices at the pump despite crude oil prices rallying more than 10% on military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya and unrest in Yemen and Bahrain. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Petroleum & Chemical Corp\uc2 . (SNP), China's top refiner by capacity, plans to process 17.7 million-17.8 million metric tons of crude oil in March, equivalent to an average of 4.19 million-4.2 million barrels/day. That represents a 3% drop from February and is the lowest level so far this year, according to energy portal Jcache. \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (PTR), the nation's second-largest refiner, plans to cut daily crude runs by 9.5% on month to around 2.7 million barrels a day, Jcache said. See REFINERY WATCH: China March Runs Likely Lower; Maintenance, High Stocks (jing.yang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds are lower late as investors take profit to switch to higher yielding assets, says a dealer with a local bank. "With Japan's nuclear situation stabilizing, risk appetite has recovered and people are shoving aside bonds," says the dealer; he thinks yields will continue to rise Wednesday on expectation of a possible domestic policy rate hike in the second half of the year. The yield on the August 2014 MGS is up 1 bp at 3.39%, the September 2016 MGS yield is up 2 bps at 3.58% and the July 2021 MGS yield is up 3 bps at 4.04%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are treading water in the absence of fresh triggers, having slipped in the previous session. Improving risk sentiment and hawkish chatter by ECB officials weighed on bunds on Monday. However, uncertainty in the Middle East continues, and \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  thinks that the risk environment remains elevated enough to prevent core government bonds from suffering massive bearish pressures. June bunds are down 0.05 at 122.12. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY extends losses to 6.5561 in the afternoon session vs 6.5611 in late Asian trade Monday. A Shanghai-based Asian bank trader tips the pair to consolidate around 6.5560 for the rest of the session; some large export settlements around lunch time drove the pair lower, he adds. "I think the PBOC intends to let the yuan rise more this week to fight inflation." The central parity was set at 6.5592 vs Monday's 6.5632. One-year USD/CNY NDFs are quoted at 6.4300/6.4350 vs 6.4350/6.4380 in late Asian trade Monday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0707 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is off 0.1% at 1507.94 in early afternoon trade but the market breadth remains positive with 443 advancers against 230 decliners, as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis ease. "Sentiment has clearly improved but a lot of the buying interest is in second-liners, which have been battered down last week," a local dealer says. He tips the index in a 1505-1515 range for the rest of the session. Among gainers \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR20.88, \uc2 SapuraCrest\uc2  (8575.KU) is up 2.8% at MYR3.62 and \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) is up 0.9% at MYR9.11. Decliners include MISC (3816.KU), which is down 3.5% at MYR7.66 and F&N (3689.KU), which is down 1.1% at MYR15.62. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is up 0.7% at 3003.74, extending gains in early afternoon trade and breaking the psychological 3000 level which it fell below last Tuesday, as risk appetite improves across the region. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  notes risk assets have made further modest gains during the Asia session, continuing the solid performance seen yesterday; "The situation at the Fukushima nuclear plant has continued to stabilize," with the Nikkei closing up 4.4% after yesterday's public holiday. However, the house adds, the situation in the Middle East remains highly volatile, but developments overnight have not been market-moving, with oil prices little changed on the day. Market volume improves slightly from yesterday at 692 million shares traded, worth S$757 million. Singapore Exchange (S68.SG), \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG) and \uc2 SembCorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) lead the index higher with gains of 3.2%, 3.9% and 3.8% respectively. Among actives, \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG) is up 0.5% at US$0.965 and \uc2 Global Logistic Properties\uc2  (MC0.SG) is up 1.7% at S$1.83. STI resistance is tipped at 3020. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore's February CPI likely rose 5.5% on-year, at the same pace as in January, according to the median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones. The continued high reading may prompt the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten again at its April review, economists say. "While inflation looks increasingly broad-based as service costs start creeping higher within the CPI basket, Chinese New Year demand is likely to have been another factor driving up the prices of food and services such as personal care services and travel packages in February," says Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew. February's CPI likely rose 0.4% on-month seasonally-adjusted, slower than the 1.3% rise in January, according to a median estimate of five economists. On-month, the measure likely eased because of lower prices for certificates of entitlement to own vehicles, another economist says. The CPI data are due Wednesday at 0500 GMT. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0401ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m00065\par }\page