ive with 443 advancers against 230 decliners, as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis ease. "Sentiment has clearly improved but a lot of the buying interest is in second-liners, which have been battered down last week," a local dealer says. He tips the index in a 1505-1515 range for the rest of the session. Among gainers \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR20.88, \uc2 SapuraCrest\uc2  (8575.KU) is up 2.8% at MYR3.62 and \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) is up 0.9% at MYR9.11. Decliners include MISC (3816.KU), which is down 3.5% at MYR7.66 and F&N (3689.KU), which is down 1.1% at MYR15.62. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is up 0.7% at 3003.74, extending gains in early afternoon trade and breaking the psychological 3000 level which it fell below last Tuesday, as risk appetite improves across the region. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  notes risk assets have made further modest gains during the Asia session, continuing the solid performance seen yesterday; "The situation at the Fukushima nuclear plant has continued to stabilize," with the Nikkei closing up 4.4% after yesterday's public holiday. However, the house adds, the situation in the Middle East remains highly volatile, but developments overnight have not been market-moving, with oil prices little changed on the day. Market volume improves slightly from yesterday at 692 million shares traded, worth S$757 million. Singapore Exchange (S68.SG), \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG) and \uc2 SembCorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) lead the index higher with gains of 3.2%, 3.9% and 3.8% respectively. Among actives, \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG) is up 0.5% at US$0.965 and \uc2 Global Logistic Properties\uc2  (MC0.SG) is up 1.7% at S$1.83. STI resistance is tipped at 3020. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore's February CPI likely rose 5.5% on-year, at the same pace as in January, according to the median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones. The continued high reading may prompt the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten again at its April review, economists say. "While inflation looks increasingly broad-based as service costs start creeping higher within the CPI basket, Chinese New Year demand is likely to have been another factor driving up the prices of food and services such as personal care services and travel packages in February," says Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew. February's CPI likely rose 0.4% on-month seasonally-adjusted, slower than the 1.3% rise in January, according to a median estimate of five economists. On-month, the measure likely eased because of lower prices for certificates of entitlement to own vehicles, another economist says. The CPI data are due Wednesday at 0500 GMT. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mostly higher, led by a surge in Japanese stocks on signs of progress in containing the country's nuclear crisis; the Nikkei is +4.4%; the S&P/ASX is flat, the HSI is +0.7%, the Kospi is +0.5%, the Taiex is +0.6%, the Shanghai Composite is +0.3%; the STI is +0.4% and the Sensex is +1.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying by Japanese exporters pushed the JPY up slightly against USD and EUR though gains were limited as short-term investors refrained from getting onboard amid concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD/JPY is at 80.89 from 81.01 in late NY trade Monday; the EUR/JPY is at 115.10 from 115.23. The EUR/USD is at 1.4229 from 1.4225. The BOJ flooded the banking system with a record amount of cash Tuesday to ensure financial institutions have enough liquidity to cope with a spike in demand for funds following the devastating earthquake; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current-account balance will rise to Y41.76 trillion Tuesday as a result of its liquidity injections, the highest since March 31, 2004. The earthquake that hit Japan will affect near-term China-Japan trade with Japan's exports to China of certain electronic, chemical, steel and other products declining, and China's exports to Japan of garments and agriculture products also falling says China Commerce Ministry spokesman Yao Jian. RBA board member Graham Kraehe says Australia's economy will likely slow for six months in the wake of the Japanese earthquake and tsunami disaster, but reconstruction needs should ultimately boost demand for resources exports. Spot gold was at $1,428.50/oz, up $1.40 from its New York settlement Monday. May Nymex crude oil futures were down seven cents at $103.02/bbl on Globex. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0341ET ]\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is at 7.7981, a tad down from 7.7985 earlier, but up from 7.7975 late Monday. The market is quiet in rather thin volume, as investors remain on the sidelines as they await fresh catalysts, says a senior trader at a Chinese bank. "The pair has stabilized after dropping from high levels amid easing concern over the Japanese nuclear crisis," he says. He tips the pair in a 7.7960-7.7990 band. (fiona.law@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0341ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m00062\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
