 Beijing-based trader with a local insurer. "It seems the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prefers quantitative measures, such as selling bills in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and raising reserve requirement ratios, rather than interest rate hikes in containing inflation at the current stage," he says. The Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 127.54 compared with Monday's close at 127.53, and is likely to extend gains near-term as liquidity in the money market remains in excess despite the latest RRR hike. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0737 GMT [Dow Jones] China is likely to reduce its daily crude runs substantially in March from record levels last month due to maintenance and relatively high domestic fuel stocks. Refineries are also operating on thin margins, as the government waits to raise prices at the pump despite crude oil prices rallying more than 10% on military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya and unrest in Yemen and Bahrain. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Petroleum & Chemical Corp\uc2 . (SNP), China's top refiner by capacity, plans to process 17.7 million-17.8 million metric tons of crude oil in March, equivalent to an average of 4.19 million-4.2 million barrels/day. That represents a 3% drop from February and is the lowest level so far this year, according to energy portal Jcache. \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (PTR), the nation's second-largest refiner, plans to cut daily crude runs by 9.5% on month to around 2.7 million barrels a day, Jcache said. See REFINERY WATCH: China March Runs Likely Lower; Maintenance, High Stocks (jing.yang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds are lower late as investors take profit to switch to higher yielding assets, says a dealer with a local bank. "With Japan's nuclear situation stabilizing, risk appetite has recovered and people are shoving aside bonds," says the dealer; he thinks yields will continue to rise Wednesday on expectation of a possible domestic policy rate hike in the second half of the year. The yield on the August 2014 MGS is up 1 bp at 3.39%, the September 2016 MGS yield is up 2 bps at 3.58% and the July 2021 MGS yield is up 3 bps at 4.04%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are treading water in the absence of fresh triggers, having slipped in the previous session. Improving risk sentiment and hawkish chatter by ECB officials weighed on bunds on Monday. However, uncertainty in the Middle East continues, and \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  thinks that the risk environment remains elevated enough to prevent core government bonds from suffering massive bearish pressures. June bunds are down 0.05 at 122.12. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY extends losses to 6.5561 in the afternoon session vs 6.5611 in late Asian trade Monday. A Shanghai-based Asian bank trader tips the pair to consolidate around 6.5560 for the rest of the session; some large export settlements around lunch time drove the pair lower, he adds. "I think the PBOC intends to let the yuan rise more this week to fight inflation." The central parity was set at 6.5592 vs Monday's 6.5632. One-year USD/CNY NDFs are quoted at 6.4300/6.4350 vs 6.4350/6.4380 in late Asian trade Monday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0707 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is off 0.1% at 1507.94 in early afternoon trade but the market breadth remains positive with 443 advancers against 230 decliners, as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis ease. "Sentiment has clearly improved but a lot of the buying interest is in second-liners, which have been battered down last week," a local dealer says. He tips the index in a 1505-1515 range for the rest of the session. Among gainers \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR20.88, \uc2 SapuraCrest\uc2  (8575.KU) is up 2.8% at MYR3.62 and \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) is up 0.9% at MYR9.11. Decliners include MISC (3816.KU), which is down 3.5% at MYR7.66 and F&N (3689.KU), which is down 1.1% at MYR15.62. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is up 0.7% at 3003.74, extending gains in early afternoon trade and breaking the psychological 3000 level which it fell below last Tuesday, as risk appetite improves across the region. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  notes risk assets have made further modest gains during the Asia session, continuing the solid performance seen yesterday; "The situation at the Fukushima nuclear plant has continued to stabilize," with the Nikkei closing up 4.4% after yesterday's public holiday. However, the house adds, the situation in the Middle East remains highly volatile, but developments overnight have not been market-moving, with oil prices little changed on the day. Market volume improves slightly from yesterday at 692 million shares traded, worth S$757 million. Singapore Exchange (S68.SG), \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG) and \uc2 SembCorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) lead the index higher with gains of 3.2%, 3.9% and 3.8% respectively. Among actives, \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG) is up 0.5% at US$0.965 and \uc2 Global Logistic Properties\uc2  (MC0.SG) is up 1.7% at S$1.83. STI resistance is tipped at 3020. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore's February CPI likely rose 5.5% on-year, at the same pace as in January, according to the median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones. The continued high reading may prompt the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten again at its April review, economists say. "While inflation looks increasingly broad-based as service costs start creeping higher within the CPI basket, Chinese New Year demand is likely to have been another factor driving up the prices of food and services such as personal care services and travel packages in February," says Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew. February's CPI likely rose 0.4% on-month seasonally-adjusted, slower than the 1.3% rise in January, according to a median estimate of five economists. On-month, the measure likely eased because of lower prices for certificates of entitlement to own vehicles, another economist says. The CPI data are due Wednesday at 0500 GMT. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mostly higher, led by a surge in Japanese stocks on signs of progress in containing the country's nuclear crisis; the Nikkei is +4.4%; the S&P/ASX is flat, the HSI is +0.7%, the Kospi is +0.5%, the Taiex is +0.6%, the Shanghai Composite is +0.3%; the STI is +0.4% and the Sensex is +1.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying by Japanese exporters pushed the JPY up slightly against USD and EUR though gains were limited as short-term investors refrained from getting onboard amid concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD/JPY is at 80.89 from 81.01 in late NY trade Monday; the EUR/JPY is at 115.10 from 115.23. The EUR/USD is at 1.4229 from 1.4225. The BOJ flooded the banking system with a record amount of cash Tuesday to ensure financial institutions have enough liquidity to cope with a spike in demand for funds following the devastating earthquake; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current-account balance will rise to Y41.76 trillion Tuesday as a result of its liquidity injections, the highest since March 31, 2004. The earthquake that hit Japan will affect near-term China-Japan trade with Japan's exports to China of certain electronic, chemical, steel and other products declining, and China's exports to Japan of garments and agriculture products also falling says China Commerce Ministry spokesman Yao Jian. RBA board member Graham Kraehe says Australia's economy will likely slow for six months in the wake of the Japanese earthquake and tsunami disaster, but reconstruction needs should ultimately boost demand for resources exports. Spot gold was at $1,428.50/oz, up $1.40 from its New York settlement Monday. May Nymex crude oil futures were down seven cents at $103.02/bbl on Globex. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is at 7.7981, a tad down from 7.7985 earlier, but up from 7.7975 late Monday. The market is quiet in rather thin volume, as investors remain on the sidelines as they await fresh catalysts, says a senior trader at a Chinese bank. "The pair has stabilized after dropping from high levels amid easing concern over the Japanese nuclear crisis," he says. He tips the pair in a 7.7960-7.7990 band. (fiona.law@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0340ET ]\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0342 GMT misspelt BHP Billiton. The correct version is as follows:) Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is up 0.2% at 4652.9 after bouncing off the 200-day moving average near the intraday low at 4637.9. Resources and energy stocks are restraining the market after copper fell 1.2% following weak U.S. home sales data and crude oil prices failed to extend Monday's Asian session gains overnight. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) and Woodside (WPL.AU) are the biggest drags on the market, down 0.7% and 1.1% respectively. Banks are mostly still positive, with Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU), ANZ (ANZ.AU) and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up more than 0.5%. Trading volume has slowed to a trickle after a reasonable morning, which saw the index hit a 6-day high of 4677.0 before retreating. "Obviously we were priced for Armageddon last week, but I think the easy snap-back gains have probably been seen and now we will see if investors are prepared to buy on strength and bet that the Japanese nuclear crisis is over and oil prices aren't going to derail global markets," says Ben Potter, market strategist at IG Markets. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0340ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m00061\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
