es, \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG) is up 0.5% at US$0.965 and \uc2 Global Logistic Properties\uc2  (MC0.SG) is up 1.7% at S$1.83. STI resistance is tipped at 3020. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore's February CPI likely rose 5.5% on-year, at the same pace as in January, according to the median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones. The continued high reading may prompt the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten again at its April review, economists say. "While inflation looks increasingly broad-based as service costs start creeping higher within the CPI basket, Chinese New Year demand is likely to have been another factor driving up the prices of food and services such as personal care services and travel packages in February," says Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew. February's CPI likely rose 0.4% on-month seasonally-adjusted, slower than the 1.3% rise in January, according to a median estimate of five economists. On-month, the measure likely eased because of lower prices for certificates of entitlement to own vehicles, another economist says. The CPI data are due Wednesday at 0500 GMT. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mostly higher, led by a surge in Japanese stocks on signs of progress in containing the country's nuclear crisis; the Nikkei is +4.4%; the S&P/ASX is flat, the HSI is +0.7%, the Kospi is +0.5%, the Taiex is +0.6%, the Shanghai Composite is +0.3%; the STI is +0.4% and the Sensex is +1.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying by Japanese exporters pushed the JPY up slightly against USD and EUR though gains were limited as short-term investors refrained from getting onboard amid concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD/JPY is at 80.89 from 81.01 in late NY trade Monday; the EUR/JPY is at 115.10 from 115.23. The EUR/USD is at 1.4229 from 1.4225. The BOJ flooded the banking system with a record amount of cash Tuesday to ensure financial institutions have enough liquidity to cope with a spike in demand for funds following the devastating earthquake; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current-account balance will rise to Y41.76 trillion Tuesday as a result of its liquidity injections, the highest since March 31, 2004. The earthquake that hit Japan will affect near-term China-Japan trade with Japan's exports to China of certain electronic, chemical, steel and other products declining, and China's exports to Japan of garments and agriculture products also falling says China Commerce Ministry spokesman Yao Jian. RBA board member Graham Kraehe says Australia's economy will likely slow for six months in the wake of the Japanese earthquake and tsunami disaster, but reconstruction needs should ultimately boost demand for resources exports. Spot gold was at $1,428.50/oz, up $1.40 from its New York settlement Monday. May Nymex crude oil futures were down seven cents at $103.02/bbl on Globex. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is at 7.7981, a tad down from 7.7985 earlier, but up from 7.7975 late Monday. The market is quiet in rather thin volume, as investors remain on the sidelines as they await fresh catalysts, says a senior trader at a Chinese bank. "The pair has stabilized after dropping from high levels amid easing concern over the Japanese nuclear crisis," he says. He tips the pair in a 7.7960-7.7990 band. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0342 GMT misspelt BHP Billiton. The correct version is as follows:) Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is up 0.2% at 4652.9 after bouncing off the 200-day moving average near the intraday low at 4637.9. Resources and energy stocks are restraining the market after copper fell 1.2% following weak U.S. home sales data and crude oil prices failed to extend Monday's Asian session gains overnight. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) and Woodside (WPL.AU) are the biggest drags on the market, down 0.7% and 1.1% respectively. Banks are mostly still positive, with Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU), ANZ (ANZ.AU) and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up more than 0.5%. Trading volume has slowed to a trickle after a reasonable morning, which saw the index hit a 6-day high of 4677.0 before retreating. "Obviously we were priced for Armageddon last week, but I think the easy snap-back gains have probably been seen and now we will see if investors are prepared to buy on strength and bet that the Japanese nuclear crisis is over and oil prices aren't going to derail global markets," says Ben Potter, market strategist at IG Markets. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0338ET ]\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Pakistan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep its key interest rate unchanged for the second successive meeting as it seeks to support a wobbly economy hurt by the unprecedented floods in July; all 15 economists polled by Dow Jones expect the \uc2 State Bank of Pakistan\uc2  to keep the discount rate, its main lending rate, unchanged at 14% at its next monetary policy review on March 26. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets every two months to review the policy and last met on Jan. 29 when it kept rates on hold after raising the lending rate three times since July. "Headline inflation has eased sharply thanks to a significant decline in the price of perishable food items and government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in domestic oil prices," said Nauman Khan, research analyst at Topline Securities in Karachi. (djn.in@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0338ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m00060\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
