S yield is up 2 bps at 3.58% and the July 2021 MGS yield is up 3 bps at 4.04%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are treading water in the absence of fresh triggers, having slipped in the previous session. Improving risk sentiment and hawkish chatter by ECB officials weighed on bunds on Monday. However, uncertainty in the Middle East continues, and \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  thinks that the risk environment remains elevated enough to prevent core government bonds from suffering massive bearish pressures. June bunds are down 0.05 at 122.12. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY extends losses to 6.5561 in the afternoon session vs 6.5611 in late Asian trade Monday. A Shanghai-based Asian bank trader tips the pair to consolidate around 6.5560 for the rest of the session; some large export settlements around lunch time drove the pair lower, he adds. "I think the PBOC intends to let the yuan rise more this week to fight inflation." The central parity was set at 6.5592 vs Monday's 6.5632. One-year USD/CNY NDFs are quoted at 6.4300/6.4350 vs 6.4350/6.4380 in late Asian trade Monday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0707 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is off 0.1% at 1507.94 in early afternoon trade but the market breadth remains positive with 443 advancers against 230 decliners, as concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis ease. "Sentiment has clearly improved but a lot of the buying interest is in second-liners, which have been battered down last week," a local dealer says. He tips the index in a 1505-1515 range for the rest of the session. Among gainers \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR20.88, \uc2 SapuraCrest\uc2  (8575.KU) is up 2.8% at MYR3.62 and \uc2 Sime Darby\uc2  (4197.KU) is up 0.9% at MYR9.11. Decliners include MISC (3816.KU), which is down 3.5% at MYR7.66 and F&N (3689.KU), which is down 1.1% at MYR15.62. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is up 0.7% at 3003.74, extending gains in early afternoon trade and breaking the psychological 3000 level which it fell below last Tuesday, as risk appetite improves across the region. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  notes risk assets have made further modest gains during the Asia session, continuing the solid performance seen yesterday; "The situation at the Fukushima nuclear plant has continued to stabilize," with the Nikkei closing up 4.4% after yesterday's public holiday. However, the house adds, the situation in the Middle East remains highly volatile, but developments overnight have not been market-moving, with oil prices little changed on the day. Market volume improves slightly from yesterday at 692 million shares traded, worth S$757 million. Singapore Exchange (S68.SG), \uc2 Golden Agri-Resources\uc2  (E5H.SG) and \uc2 SembCorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) lead the index higher with gains of 3.2%, 3.9% and 3.8% respectively. Among actives, \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG) is up 0.5% at US$0.965 and \uc2 Global Logistic Properties\uc2  (MC0.SG) is up 1.7% at S$1.83. STI resistance is tipped at 3020. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore's February CPI likely rose 5.5% on-year, at the same pace as in January, according to the median forecast of 12 economists polled by Dow Jones. The continued high reading may prompt the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten again at its April review, economists say. "While inflation looks increasingly broad-based as service costs start creeping higher within the CPI basket, Chinese New Year demand is likely to have been another factor driving up the prices of food and services such as personal care services and travel packages in February," says Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew. February's CPI likely rose 0.4% on-month seasonally-adjusted, slower than the 1.3% rise in January, according to a median estimate of five economists. On-month, the measure likely eased because of lower prices for certificates of entitlement to own vehicles, another economist says. The CPI data are due Wednesday at 0500 GMT. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mostly higher, led by a surge in Japanese stocks on signs of progress in containing the country's nuclear crisis; the Nikkei is +4.4%; the S&P/ASX is flat, the HSI is +0.7%, the Kospi is +0.5%, the Taiex is +0.6%, the Shanghai Composite is +0.3%; the STI is +0.4% and the Sensex is +1.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying by Japanese exporters pushed the JPY up slightly against USD and EUR though gains were limited as short-term investors refrained from getting onboard amid concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD/JPY is at 80.89 from 81.01 in late NY trade Monday; the EUR/JPY is at 115.10 from 115.23. The EUR/USD is at 1.4229 from 1.4225. The BOJ flooded the banking system with a record amount of cash Tuesday to ensure financial institutions have enough liquidity to cope with a spike in demand for funds following the devastating earthquake; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current-account balance will rise to Y41.76 trillion Tuesday as a result of its liquidity injections, the highest since March 31, 2004. The earthquake that hit Japan will affect near-term China-Japan trade with Japan's exports to China of certain electronic, chemical, steel and other products declining, and China's exports to Japan of garments and agriculture products also falling says China Commerce Ministry spokesman Yao Jian. RBA board member Graham Kraehe says Australia's economy will likely slow for six months in the wake of the Japanese earthquake and tsunami disaster, but reconstruction needs should ultimately boost demand for resources exports. Spot gold was at $1,428.50/oz, up $1.40 from its New York settlement Monday. May Nymex crude oil futures were down seven cents at $103.02/bbl on Globex. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is at 7.7981, a tad down from 7.7985 earlier, but up from 7.7975 late Monday. The market is quiet in rather thin volume, as investors remain on the sidelines as they await fresh catalysts, says a senior trader at a Chinese bank. "The pair has stabilized after dropping from high levels amid easing concern over the Japanese nuclear crisis," he says. He tips the pair in a 7.7960-7.7990 band. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0342 GMT misspelt BHP Billiton. The correct version is as follows:) Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is up 0.2% at 4652.9 after bouncing off the 200-day moving average near the intraday low at 4637.9. Resources and energy stocks are restraining the market after copper fell 1.2% following weak U.S. home sales data and crude oil prices failed to extend Monday's Asian session gains overnight. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) and Woodside (WPL.AU) are the biggest drags on the market, down 0.7% and 1.1% respectively. Banks are mostly still positive, with Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU), ANZ (ANZ.AU) and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up more than 0.5%. Trading volume has slowed to a trickle after a reasonable morning, which saw the index hit a 6-day high of 4677.0 before retreating. "Obviously we were priced for Armageddon last week, but I think the easy snap-back gains have probably been seen and now we will see if investors are prepared to buy on strength and bet that the Japanese nuclear crisis is over and oil prices aren't going to derail global markets," says Ben Potter, market strategist at IG Markets. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Pakistan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep its key interest rate unchanged for the second successive meeting as it seeks to support a wobbly economy hurt by the unprecedented floods in July; all 15 economists polled by Dow Jones expect the \uc2 State Bank of Pakistan\uc2  to keep the discount rate, its main lending rate, unchanged at 14% at its next monetary policy review on March 26. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets every two months to review the policy and last met on Jan. 29 when it kept rates on hold after raising the lending rate three times since July. "Headline inflation has eased sharply thanks to a significant decline in the price of perishable food items and government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in domestic oil prices," said Nauman Khan, research analyst at Topline Securities in Karachi. (djn.in@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0337ET ]\par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI extends gains in the afternoon and it's up 0.6% at 22,819.65; in the short run, the index may target psychological 23,000, where the HSI's 10-day moving average lies. Volume stays low at HK$42.21 billion. Overall, the HK market is rebounding for the 3rd straight session; South China Research says provided Japan's nuclear crisis continues to ease and the situation in Libya does not deteriorate, the HSI should remain buoyant today. Top 3 blue-chip percentage gainers are \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK), \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK) and Cosco Pacific (1199.HK), as bargain-hunters are stepping in, while China Life (2628.HK) is up 0.9% at HK$29.20 ahead of FY10 results expected later in the day. (robert.li@dowjones.com) [ 03-22-11 0337ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110322e73m0005z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
