\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,101 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: EXPLOSION AT DAIICHI NO. 3 FUKUSHIMA PLANT; 6 INJURED, 500 EVACUATED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An explosion hit the No. 3 nuclear reactor building at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s Daiichi plant in Fukushima Prefecture Monday around 0201 GMT. Six people were injured and 500 were evacuated from the surrounding area. (published 0419 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ OFFERS TO INJECT ANOTHER Y3 TLN SAME-DAY FUNDS INTO MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan Monday offered to inject another Y3 trillion of same-day funds into the short-term money market, following its announcement in the morning it would inject a record Y12 trillion. (published 0429 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: BOJ OFFERS TO INJECT RECORD Y7 TRILLION SAME-DAY FUNDS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan Monday offered to inject a record Y7 trillion of same-day funds into the short-term money market, aiming to secure market confidence after the massive earthquake and tsunami that struck the nation last Friday. (published 0142 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: ECONOMIC IMPACT OF JAPAN QUAKE BIGGER THAN INITIALLY THOUGHT -REUTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Investor Service said the economic impact from Japan's earthquake and tsunami is worse than initially expected and that Tokyo will likely overshoot its borrowing target to pay for reconstruction, Reuters said Monday. (published 0329 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN YOSANO: CONVINCED MARKETS WILL CALM DOWN AS JAPAN FUNDAMENTALS FIRM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yosano also said he sees no need to change the government's view on the Japanese economy, saying the fundamentals of the economy are still healthy. (published 0202 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN MOF OFFICIAL: TO TAKE DECISIVE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  STEPS IF NEEDED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior Japanese finance ministry official threatened to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to stem the yen's rises Monday, saying authorities will take "decisive steps" if necessary. (published 2315 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ NOT CONDUCTING 'RATE CHECKS' IN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET TODAY - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DEALER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan hasn't been conducting "rate checks" in the Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Monday, dealers said, indicating the authorities aren't preparing imminent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the strong yen. (published 0431 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CHINA\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  NPC: PREMIER WEN: CONFIDENT OF MANAGING INFLATION EXPECTATIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao said Monday that China is confident of successfully managing inflation expectations, and will continue with gradual reform of the yuan's exchange rate. (published 0543 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: CHINA PREMIER SAYS TO EXPAND FLEXIBILITY OF YUAN EXCHANGE RATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao Monday said the government will continue to expand the flexibility of the yuan's exchange rate, but said any change needs to be measured because of its potentially broad economic impact. (published 0351 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA NPC: CHINA PREMIER WEN: LOWER GROWTH TARGET SHOWS DETERMINATION TO RESTRUCTURE ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao said Monday the country's lower economic growth target for the next five years shows Beijing's determination in changing the economy's structure but cautioned the government also needs to seek a balance between maintaining growth and combating inflation. (published 0241 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA FEBRUARY LENDING SLOWS IN SIGN TIGHTENING MEASURES ARE WORKING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Banks in China reined in new lending in February, while money supply growth slowed, a clear sign the government's monetary tightening efforts are working and further measures to cool the economy may not be necessary for now, economists said. (published 0514 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA'S ECONOMY FACES RISK OF VOLATILITY IF INFLATION RATE REMAINS HIGH - S&P \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's economy faces the risk of significant volatility if the inflation rate remains high, as inflation concerns could lead to a policy over-reaction, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services said in a note Monday. (published 0522 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA JANUARY-FEBRUARY FISCAL REVENUE CNY1.849 TLN; UP 36% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's fiscal revenue in January and February totalled CNY1.849 trillion, up 36% from a year earlier, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday. (published 0230 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA NPC: CHINA BANKING REGULATOR: JANUARY-FEBRUARY LOAN GROWTH 17.7% TO 17.9% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese banks had loan growth of 17.7% to 17.9% in the first two months of the year compared to a year earlier, Liu Mingkang, chairman of the China Banking Regulatory Commission, said Monday. (published 0148 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: ECONOMISTS SEE US JOBLESS RATE AT 7.7% AT NOV 2012 ELECTION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. jobless rate will be 7.7% in November 2012, the highest level for a presidential election month since Jimmy Carter defeated Gerald Ford in 1976, according to the average forecast of economists in the latest Wall Street Journal survey. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVERNMENT: JAPAN QUAKE'S NEGATIVE IMPACT ON KOREA ECONOMY LIMITED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government said Monday it expects Japan's massive earthquake to have a limited impact on the local economy for the time being, but prolonged disruptions in logistics and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at major Japanese firms could hurt Korea's procurement of key components and eventually be detrimental to its exports. (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : NO EXPLICIT SIGN OF RISK AVERSION AFTER JAPAN'S QUAKE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees no explicit sign of risk aversion in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market after Japan's earthquake and tsunami, Assistant Governor Pongpen Ruengvirayudh said Monday. (published 0536 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI COMPANIES URGED TO INVEST IN JAPAN AFTER QUAKE, TSUNAMI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japan External Trade Organization on Monday urged Thai companies to invest in Japan as the north Asian country continued to battle through the aftermath of last week's earthquake and tsunami. (published 0557 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO FACTOR JAPAN QUAKE IN POLICY DECISION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is closely watching the impact of Friday's earthquake in Japan on global trade and commodity prices, noting that any such fallout will have a bearing on its policy decision later this month as well as the country's external payments position. (published 0208 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NZ FEBRUARY MEDIAN HOUSE PRICE NZ$350,000 VS NZ$340,000 JANUARY - REINZ \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand home prices and sales rose in February from January, data from the Real Estate Institute of New Zealand showed Monday. (published 2218 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par HEARD ON THE STREET: JAPAN'S QUAKE COULD SHAKE UP REINSURERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A disaster for Japan might not be the disaster for reinsurers that Friday's share price falls seemed to suggest. True, some of the heaviest losses from Friday's earthquake, the fifth biggest recorded globally since 1900, are likely to be felt by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Munich Re\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss Re\f28 \uc2 \f2 , two of the world's largest players. Earnings downgrades could lie ahead this year. But longer term, it could reduce surplus capital, paving the way for higher premium prices. (published 0530 GMT) [ 14-03-11 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110314e73e000fg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
