\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Bank Group Economist: Euro Jitters To Reoccur Until Debt Crisis Resolved\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ian Talley Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Market skittishness should continue to weaken the euro until European Union officials comprehensively resolve their debt crisis, a senior economist at an association of the world's largest international banks said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 'The jitters we've seen over the past few days will reoccur and reoccur and reoccur until these structural problems are resolved comprehensively, not just at the EU level, but also at the national level,' said Jeremy Lawson, deputy director of global macroeconomics at the Institute of International Finance.\par \par \uc2 Lawson told Dow Jones Newswires that the euro's troubles could worsen if officials failed to establish a decisive plan to fix their sovereign debt, budget and growth ailments at a summit scheduled for the end of March.\par \par \uc2 'Obviously, if that settlement does sort of fall short of expectations, then there's certainly the risk of more turmoil,' he said on the sidelines of a conference in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The euro strengthened in recent weeks as officials in Europe raised market expectations that they could make major headway in boosting their firepower and the flexibility of its bailout fund and make a pact on fiscal discipline. But as officials have so far failed to overcome their political differences, markets have become increasingly skeptical about the ability of E.U. members to reach a quality deal.\par \par \uc2 The downgrading of Spain's credit rating Thursday was a good reminder to officials that the structural weaknesses within the euro zone must be fixed, Lawson said.\par \par \uc2 And while Portugal is still able to get funding access in the markets, the cost it is paying to borrow to fund its refinancing needs, compared to its anemic growth potential, isn't sustainable, he said. 'It's not clear that there's a plan for the yields to come down at this stage...and we see there's a substantial risk that some sort of plan specific to Portugal is going to be necessary,' he said.\par \par \uc2 'What the scope of that is is going to really depend on what's agreed at the European level,' Lawson added, adding that the terms were unlikely to please Portugal.\par \par \uc2 Turning to emerging markets, Lawson warned that the use of capital controls and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets--attempts to curb investment flows and ease pressures on their economies, particularly as inflation takes root--are likely sowing seeds for economic damage later.\par \par \uc2 'A number of governments are almost trying to escape the inevitable...these stop-gap measures are unlikely to be successful and you just put off the day of reckoning,' Lawson said.\par \par \uc2 For example, Brazil has instituted a raft of measures meant to add friction to the mass of money flowing into their burgeoning economy. Also the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  daily has been buying dollars to keep a lid on its exchange rate. But Lawson warned there are costs associated with those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While acknowledging that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of keeping its yuan pegged at a rate many believe is substantially undervalued, the IIF economist said Brazilian authorities may be creating new headaches for the country with some of these policies.\par \par \uc2 'If there are domestic imbalances, putting those off generates additional inflation risks, additional asset and credit risks...and makes the eventual correction worse,' including in the value of its exchange rate, Lawson said.\par \par \uc2 If the country is going to use controls, authorities need to ensure that they change the distribution of the capital flows rather than stemming them in aggregate, he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Ian Talley, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9285; ian.talley@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110312e73b000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
