\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -China deficit, dovish BoK hit won; more pressure seen\b0\par\par\uc2 874 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Won down 0.5 pct; BoK's future tightening seen modest\par \par \uc2  * China deficit spur Asian-wide dlr short covering\par \par \uc2  * Baht gives up gains on BoT \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  data \par \par \uc2 (Updates with text, prices)\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 10 (Reuters) - The South Korean won hit a one-week low against the dollar, leading overall falls in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday as  China's surprise trade deficit and less hawkish comments by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted investors to cover dollar short positions.\par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea raised interest rates as expected but hinted it would not accelerte policy tightening despite stronger-than-expected inflation. \par \par \uc2  That could cause investors to sell the won for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia where central banks are more keen to rein in prices, such as Singapore, analysts said.\par \par \uc2  "I prefer SGD and MYR as people will seek \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries where policymakers are proactively fighting inflation," said Perry Kojodjojo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2  Singapore is widely expected to tighten in April.\par \par \uc2  Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen keeping rates steady on Friday, a Reuters poll showed, but the country remains relatively shielded by price controls and food and fuel subsidies. \par \par \uc2  Still, the Singapore dollar and the Malaysian ringgit also suffered from dollar short coverings after data showed China swung to a surprise trade deficit in February of $7.3 billion, its largest in seven years. \par \par \uc2  The data provided more reason for Asian central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, said Frances Cheung at Credit Agricole CIB in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2  "The China data outcome does put an alert to policy makers in the region that they should be more aggressive in curbing local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength."\par \par \uc2  WON\par \par \uc2  The won  turned lower against the dollar as investors at home and abroad covered dollar short positions after the Bank of Korea raised interest rates by 25 basis points (bps) as expected.\par \par \uc2  Some investors had bet the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would raise its base rates by 50 bps.\par \par \uc2  Those players had to quickly cover dollar short positions, putting more pressure on the won, although exporters provided some relief, especially when the won weakened past 1,120, they added.\par \par \uc2  The won weakened to as soft as 1,122.4 per dollar, the weakest since March 3.\par \par \uc2  "Short coverings on a 50-bps rate hike expectation has been completed, but we are faced with fresh problems from China data and European debt issues," said a senior European bank dealer in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  "The range is raised to 1,115-1,125 from 1,110-1,120," he added.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar  hit an over one-week low against the dollar as investors rushed to cover dollar short positions spooked by China's trade deficit.\par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar weakened to as soft as 1.2717 versus the U.S. Dollar, the weakest since March 2.\par \par \uc2  BAHT\par \par \uc2  The Thai baht erased much of its gains on dollar short coverings. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the baht hovered around an over two-month low although Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying dollars to check the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains.\par \par \uc2  The baht strengthened to as firm as 30.18 per dollar, the strongest since Jan. 7.\par \par \uc2   It has room to gain to 30.12, the 61.8 percent Fibonacci retracement of its November-January rise.\par \par \uc2  The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in markets, with traders estimating it bought $350 million on Wednesday as the baht has risen almost 2 percent versus the greenback since February.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR                                  \par \par \uc2  Change on the day at 0605 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        82.83          82.73       -0.12\par  Sing dlr        1.2701         1.2679       -0.17\par  Taiwan dlr      29.480         29.395       -0.29\par  Korean won     1121.60        1115.60       -0.53\par  Baht             30.28          30.31       +0.10\par  Peso             43.47          43.42       -0.12\par  Rupiah         8778.00        8780.00       +0.02\par  Rupee            45.08          45.00       -0.18\par  Ringgit         3.0360         3.0320       -0.13\par  Yuan            6.5728         6.5721       -0.01\par                                                                                                \par  Change so far in 2011                                                                      \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen        82.83          81.15       -2.03\par  Sing dlr        1.2701         1.2820       +0.94\par  Taiwan dlr      29.480         30.368       +3.0