\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Up Late After Wen Reiterates Gradual  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reform\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2011\par 10:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity    Pvs \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.5752               6.5757 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.5716    -0.05%     6.5750 \par              High        6.5780    +0.04% \par              Low         6.5713    -0.06% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan rose slightly against the U.S. dollar late Monday after Premier Wen Jiabao reiterated the need for a more flexible yuan and said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reform will be carried out gradually and carefully.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The lower central parity rate set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday reflects Wen's comments, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 On the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.5716 around 0830 GMT, down from CNY6.5750 late Friday. It traded between CNY6.5713 and CNY6.5780.\par \par \uc2 At CNY6.5716 to the dollar, the yuan has risen 3.9% against the U.S. unit since June, when China effectively ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two-year-long peg to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  fixed the dollar-yuan central parity rate lower at 6.5752, though it was nearly unchanged from Friday's 6.5757, which traders generally interpreted as a clear signal Beijing won't allow any immediate sharp yuan appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "The PBOC chose not to set a record-low fixing today. Combined with Wen's comments, that likely means Beijing will guide the yuan in a steady manner for at least the next two weeks, ahead of and during the National People's Congress and Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference," a Shenzhen-based local bank trader said.\par \par \uc2 In an online chat with Internet users Sunday, Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao reiterated China's stance that the reform of the exchange rate regime should be carried out independently, gradually, carefully and controllably for the purpose of social stability, as many Chinese processing enterprises will go bankrupt if the yuan were to rise substantially at once.\par \par \uc2 China will continue to promote the yuan's flexibility based on market demand, and will shift the yuan's peg to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the dollar, Wen added.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said they continue to expect China to allow the yuan to rise about 5% this year, in part to fight inflation. China's prices are expected to rise further in February from January's 4.9% rise, economists have said.\par \par \uc2 PBOC Vice Gov. Yi Gang blamed external imbalances and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the "root cause" of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation problem in comments over the weekend, the first time inflation has been officially linked to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large current account and capital account surplus have created massive inflows of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The PBOC buys up those inflows by issuing yuan, thus swelling the money supply and adding to inflation pressures, Yi said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said they will keep an eye on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's central parity rates this week, as the fixings will signal how serious Beijing is about a stronger yuan.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards fell to 6.4140/6.4180 from 6.4240/6.4340 late Friday, implying a 2.5% rise by the yuan against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year.\par \par \uc2 -By Jean Yung, Dow Jones Newswires; 8621 6120-1200; jean.yung@dowjones.com [ 28-02-11 0927GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110228e72s000r8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
