\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK: Predicted Ranges, Themes-2-\b0\par\par\uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2011\par 02:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 USD/CNY downward bias. Premier Wen Jiabao's downgrading of China's annual growth targets to 7% for the next 5 years will get excessive focus by the market but this needs to be taken in context of other policy-maker statements. First, Wen also said CPI inflation needs to be controlled via liquidity control and reiterated the need for greater CNY flexibility. Also, PBOC vice governor Yi Gang has said external surpluses in addition to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were the "root cause" of the current inflation problem. No mention at all of interest rate hikes or overheating, and the attribution of inflation (partly) to liquidity is new. While "slower growth" will be taken by many to mean overheating, the data still don't support that notion and Wen's comments should be taken in the context of economic restructuring and the sacrificing of faster export growth for the slower engines of consumption and investment.\par \par \uc2  In that context, caution on rate hikes continues to be warranted, with CNY appreciation and RRR hikes (and other quantitative measures) remaining the primary anti-inflation tools. For the week, the PBOC is likely to maintain a downward trajectory on the USD/CNY central parity settings to reinforce these statements and the market will probably come around to focusing on that in short order. Dow Jones technical analysis shows USD/CNY 1-year NDF consolidating in the 6.5560-6.3840 range; NDF was last at 6.4230 vs spot at 6.5740. Spot USD/CNY has immediate support at 6.5417 (trendline from Oct. 15 low), below at 6.5140 (bottom edge of trend channel from Dec. 20 high). Initial resistance at 6.5850 (trendline from Dec. 20 high), before 6.6017 (Jan. 31 high), then 6.6077 (55-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP biased higher on risk aversion. 'The market is likely to continue to consolidate with upside bias until after the situation in the Middle East is resolved,' says a local bank trader, who tips the pair in a 43,50-43.90 band today as the market watches for overseas cues. No such resolution in the Middle East is in sight and with the oil price now surging, USD/PHP may rise further as corporate USD-demand starts to emerge to secure oil supplies. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate resistance is at 44.75 (Jan. 18 high), then 45.00 (round figure) and 45.58 (Aug. 16 peak). Support at 43.00 (big figure), then 42.34 (Nov. 4 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD to range trade. The market seems to be largely sanguine about the pair, with \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  saying, 'we would have to see oil prices rising more than 50% from the current level to see Taiwan''''s current account surplus at risk;' it added Taiwan''''s large non-oil goods trade and income account surpluses kept the current account balance in surplus even when oil prices set record highs in 2008. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  also said Taiwan''''s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  'sounded more open to (using) New Taiwan dollar strength' to combat rising inflation, mostly caused by rising import prices on food and energy. But in the near term, the pair won''''t escape the gravitational pull of regional outflows and a local trader tips the pair remaining in a 29.50-30.00 band this week. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support at 28.630 (13-year low) before 28.400 (trendline from Aug. 4 low), then 28.000 (psychological). Initial resistance is at before 30.000 (big figure), then 30.950 (Jan.12 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB may edge higher. Although the THB is not one of the most vulnerable in Asia to high oil prices, government officials have been jawboning in preemptive attempts to placate the markets. However, the THB will be susceptible to risk aversion given its domestic political tensions. A Bangkok-based dealer tips USD/THB will stick to a 30.60-30.65 range early this week, with the geopolitical uncertainties set to keep the pair fairly steady, with most investors adopting a cautious approach. Dow Jones technical analysis shows USD/THB has immediate resistance at 31.19 (Jan. 31 high), then 32.00 (July 20 low, bottom of previous base). Support at 30.32 (Jan. 19 low), before 30.00 (big figure) then 29.50 (Nov. 20 low).\par \par \uc2 USD/HKD range bound. Risk aversion should continue to impede inflows and the market''''s kneejerk reaction to Chinese Premier Wen announcing a lower annual economic growth target of 7% from the 7.5% currently, could also boost USD/HKD. But on the flipside, Wen called again for more CNY flexibility and PBOC vice governor Yi''''s comments about external imbalances and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  needing to be reduced point toward a lower USD/CNY, and drag down USD/HKD in its wake. Hedge fund manager Paul Tudor Jones says the U.S. could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the USD/HKD to send a message to \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the USD/CNY and USD/HKD are closely intertwined. The problem with this is argument is that it''''s the dog wagging the tail - neither the U.S. nor the USD/HKD is going to move the USD/CNY. Public attacks like the suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only provoke a backlash from Beijing. Dow Jones technical analysis shows initial support at 7.7798 (Feb. 7 low), then 7.7729 (200-day moving average), below at 7.7666 (Jan. 4 low). Initial resistance is at 7.7984 (Jan. 24 high) before 7.8062 (June 8 high).\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110301e72s0019r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pi