\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE:PBOC Yi: External Surpluses Root Cause Of Current Inflation Problem\b0\par\par\uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2011\par 02:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds Yi's direct quotes, the figure of hot money that inflow into China.)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large external imbalances, combined with its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, are the "root cause" of the country's current inflation problem, Yi Gang, vice governor of the People's Bank of China, said Saturday.\par \par \uc2 China should increase the flexibility of the yuan exchange rate and undertake a number of internal structural changes to reduce its trade surplus, Yi said, adding that China's focus should be on boosting imports rather than suppressing exports.\par \par \uc2 In a wide-ranging lecture at a university in Beijing, Yi also rejected many criticisms often heard in China of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its investment of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, saying it would be difficult to diversify the reserves into commodities or other alternative assets.\par \par \uc2 China's large "twin surpluses" in the current account and capital account have created massive inflows of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The PBOC buys up those inflows by issuing newly minted yuan, thus swelling the money supply and adding to inflation pressures, Yi said.\par \par \uc2 "Why do we have so much base money? Why is the money supply relatively loose? A major reason is that our surpluses are too large," Yi said. "To maintain the basic stability of the yuan exchange rate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows. If it didn't, the yuan wouldn't be so stable."\par \par \uc2 In addition to boosting the flexibility of the yuan exchange rate, China also should adjust resource prices to address imbalances, he said, as many resources are still traded in China at below their natural prices. China also should boost wages and social benefits to lift consumption, step up its enforcement of environment regulations and undertake other structural reforms to address imbalances.\par \par \uc2 After the PBOC buys up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows, it attempts to "sterilize" or offset the newly created money supply by ordering banks to hold more funds in reserve and by issuing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills. But Yi said such sterilization \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  have costs to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it must pay interest to banks on the bills and the reserve funds that they park with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We have been undertaking sterilization efforts to lock up excessive liquidity and maintain price stability," Yi said. "But all these actions have costs. We have to take into account the marginal costs and benefits if this state of affairs persists."\par \par \uc2 Many in China argue that inflows of "hot money," short-term capital that seeks to benefit from yuan appreciation and interest-rate arbitrage, are also a main factor behind China's foreign reserve accumulation and a source of imported inflation pressures.\par \par \uc2 However, estimates released last week by the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 --a unit of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that Yi also heads--showed such hot money inflows to be relatively limited, with just $35.5 billion of net inflows last year. Yi said the figures demonstrate that hot money inflows account for a small part of total reserve accumulation and that China's current and capital account surpluses are the main factor.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts inside and outside of China said SAFE's figures likely underestimate the true level of hot money inflows, as many such inflows are deliberately disguised as ordinary current account transactions. Yi acknowledged that this takes place to a certain degree but defended the overall accuracy of SAFE's estimates. Estimates by foreign countries such as the U.S. of their deficits with China are even greater than China's estimates, he said, indicating that China's estimates can't be significantly understated.\par \par \uc2 Many Chinese critics of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  argue that China should diversify its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves away from sovereign debt--U.S. Treasury bonds in particular--and toward other assets such as commodities or even land. Yi said it is difficult for China to diversity into such assets without having a large impact on their market price. -By Aaron Back, Dow Jones Newswires; 8610 8400 7799; aaron.back@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110301e72s0019a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
