\par\par \uc2 Interview\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Ex-PBOC Adviser: Pace Of Yuan Rise Inadequate To Fight Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Davis  \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL  \par \uc2 1,007 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2011\par 11:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 On the eve of an international conference that will likely focus on China's exchange rate policies, a prominent Chinese academic said that the rate of yuan appreciation is inadequate to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 "From China's point of view, appreciation is beneficial," said Yu Yongding, a senior economist at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences. "We have a trade surplus of about $200 billion and a capital account surplus about $100 billion. But what use is it if we can only buy (U.S.) government securities at a very low rate of return and risk large capital losses in the future," he said in an interview with the Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese government said its trade surplus was $183 billion in 2010.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yu's comments are closely watched, in part, because he was a member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary advisory committee from 2004 to 2007 and has kept a high profile since then.\par \par \uc2 China has been letting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate at an annual rate of about 6% against the dollar since mid June when it allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float somewhat. But when accounting for the different levels of inflation between the two countries, the yuan is rising at about a 10% a year.\par \par \uc2 That exchange rate policy is likely to be a focus on finance ministers and central bankers of the Group of 20 major powers, who are meeting in Paris beginning on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Yu didn't specify what level of the yuan he thought was appropriate. Rather he said that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and that would tend to boost the value of the yuan against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Yuan appreciation "will help contain inflation," Mr. Yu said because it would make imports cheaper in dollar terms. He said the choice facing China now isn't whether there will be appreciation of the yuan, but whether the change in the yuan's value comes through changes in the nominal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate or through inflation.\par \par \uc2 That's an argument U.S. Treasury Secretary Tim Geithner has been using too. Thus far, it has been dismissed by the Chinese government, which worries about the effects of yuan appreciation on Chinese industry.\par \par \uc2 China's export industry would be hurt, Mr. Yu said, but "we can help those employees (who lose their jobs) and those enterprises that are bankrupt. There are costs but they are affordable."\par \par \uc2 He noted that if China bought fewer U.S. government securities, as it reduced its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , long-term interest rates in the U.S. could rise, deepening U.S. economic problems.\par \par \uc2 To reduce ill effects in both countries he said, the adjustments should follow discussions by U.S. and Chinese officials who should coordinate policies.\par \par \uc2 -By Bob Davis, The Wall Street Journal; bob.davis@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 17 Feb 2011 05:52 EDT DJ INTERVIEW: Ex-PBOC Adviser: Pace Of Yuan Rise Inadequate To Fight Inflation\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Bob Davis \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the eve of an international conference that will likely focus on China's exchange rate policies, a prominent Chinese academic said that the rate of yuan appreciation is i