\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: China Resists French  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,007 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2011\par 21:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to add more background and comments from Chinese economist.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Nathalie Boschat and Andrew Browne \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 PARIS (Dow Jones)--French President Nicolas Sarkozy's ambitious plan to get the Chinese government to discuss its exchange-rate policy with other world leaders this year is getting off to a rough start, according to several international officials.\par \par \uc2 How to better coordinate global monetary policies is one of the hot-button issues on the table at a meeting of finance ministers from the Group of 20 industrialized and developing nations here on Friday and Saturday. Central to the talks is the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, which many of China's trading partners complain has been kept artificially to help the country's exports.\par \par \uc2 In a bid to overcome China's resistance to addressing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue head-on, France--which now holds the rotating presidency of the G-20--has suggested holding separate "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  seminars" throughout the year, including a first one in China at the end of March. When Sarkozy announced the initiative last year, French officials said they had secured the backing of Chinese President Hu Jintao.\par \par \uc2 But Chinese officials are now throwing cold water on the initiative by giving it less importance than the French had hoped, G-20 officials say.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Sarkozy was planning to host the China event himself, and therefore to draw other high-level officials, including finance ministers, heads of Treasury and central bankers, from around the world, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 But China, which is uncomfortable with being put under the spotlight over its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, is reluctant to discuss the issue in any international forum. Chinese officials privately indicate that they want to keep the seminar at a working level, without the political theater that would inevitably surround an event attended by ministers, or even heads of state. Beijing also doesn't want to be responsible for any of the agenda points, in order not to take ownership of any of the potential conclusions, according to one top G-20 official.\par \par \uc2 Beijing has repeatedly said it will deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -exchange issues at its own pace and in its own way; from Beijing's point of view, the worst scenario would be to face public pressure for China to appreciate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  faster coming out of a meeting on its own soil, potentially embarrassing the government in front of its own people.\par \par \uc2 The result is that no firm date, venue or participation list has been set for the event.\par \par \uc2 French officials say the format of the event, and in particular the balance between finance ministry officials and experts attending the meeting, has yet to be decided upon because of difficult logistics. They say that there is already a very busy calendar of international meetings in March and April, and that everything is otherwise proceeding to plan. A spokesman for Sarkozy's office didn't return calls or emails for comment.\par \par \uc2 China has been letting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate at an annual rate of about 6% against the dollar since mid-June when it allowed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float somewhat. Accounting for the different levels of inflation between China and the U.S., the yuan is rising at about a 10% a year. U.S. and other countries say that isn't enough. Last month, U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner said that China is fueling its own inflation by holding down the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that it is in China's own best interests to allow the yuan to rise.\par \par \uc2 A prominent Chinese academic is taking the same view. "From China's point of view, appreciation is beneficial," Yu Yongding, a senior economist at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences, said in an interview. "We have a trade surplus of about $200 billion and a capital account surplus about $100 billion. But what use is it if we can only buy [U.S.] government securities at a very low rate of return and risk large capital losses in the future."\par \par \uc2 The Chinese government said its trade surplus was $183 billion in 2010.\par \par \uc2 Yu's comments are closely watched, in part, because he was a member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary advisory committee from 2004 to 2007 and has kept a high profile since then.\par \par \uc2 Yu didn't specify what level of the yuan he thought was appropriate. Rather, he said, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reduce its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, arguing that would tend to boost the value of the yuan against the dollar. Yuan appreciation "will help contain inflation," Yu said, because it would make imports cheaper in dollar terms. The choice facing China now, added Yu, isn't whether there will be appreciation of the yuan, but whether the change in the yuan's value comes through changes in the nominal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate or through inflation.\par \par \uc2 At this week's G-20 talks, another controversial issue is the creation of a list of indicators to help gauge whether countries are pursuing policies that cause imbalances in the world economy. That list of indicators would then be used to lay out actual benchmarks to quantify dangerous economic distortions, such as when excessive reserves build up or exchange-rate policies are provoking instability. China is opposed to such benchmarks as it could censure Beijing for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 The list is likely to include measures for current-account surpluses or deficits. But several other emerging markets, in addition to China, also want \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related indicators-- such as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and the real effective exchange rate--to be excluded from the list.\par \par \uc2 "Some countries do not like to be identified as pursuing such and such policy," French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde said at an international finance meeting in Paris on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 --Bob Davis, David Gauthier-Villars and Ian Talley contributed to this article. [ 02-17-11 1516ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110217e72h000lm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
