\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: China's Rate Increase Is Notice It Intends To Step Up Fight Against Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bob Davis and Aaron Back   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 1,204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2011\par 18:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING--With the fireworks still echoing from China's Lunar New Year holiday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would raise interest rates for the third time in four months, putting markets on notice that it intends to further step up its fight against accelerating inflation.\par \par \uc2 The announcement by the People's Bank of China that it would lift its benchmark lending and deposit rates by 0.25 percentage point came Tuesday evening in Beijing, as many businesses prepared to open for business after the weeklong holiday. The PBOC said it would raise the one-year yuan lending rate to 6.06% from 5.81%, and the one-year yuan deposit rate to 3.00% from 2.75%, effective Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 China and other large emerging nations are using a panoply of policy measures to battle inflation fueled by a global rise in food and commodity prices, but few have gotten a firm grip on the problem, and more interest rate hikes are anticipated. Emerging nations also have seen a surge in investment from abroad in property and other assets, as interest rates in the U.S., Europe and Japan remain at near-zero levels. In Asia, several \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have rallied to multiyear highs, and authorities in South Korea and Malaysia have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Qu Hongbin, co-head of Asian economics research at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , said he expects another quarter-point rate hike in China and "at least" another 1.5 percentage-point increase in bank reserve requirements by the end of June as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  carries on its inflation fight.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's hike came after Asian markets closed, but it weighed on stocks in Europe and rattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodity markets. Global markets have fretted that central banks in fast-growing economies could make a concerted interest-rate push to try to catch up with inflation-risking a sudden slowdown in global economic growth, which now relies heavily on emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Marc Ostwald, an analyst at London-based Monument Securities, said that while the rate hike may lead to temporary jitters that growth will be slowed in China and other emerging economies, underlying momentum for those economies remains strong. "While equity markets may continue to correct, at some stage it will dawn on many investors that their worst fears for developing market assets due to policy tightening are not being realized," he said.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts have been predicting since China's last rate hike was announced on Dec. 25 that another was likely by March. Senior Chinese officials have reinforced those expectations with tough talk. Shortly before the Feb. 2-8 Lunar New Year break, PBOC Governor Zhou Xiaochuan warned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would focus on inflation. He pointed out in an interview on Jan. 30 that while Chinese price growth slowed slightly in December, it was stronger than many had forecast and signaled it had more room to climb.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  have warned that China's inflation, which had eased to 4.6% in December, compared to a year earlier, was likely to top 5% in January. That's because of rising food and housing prices. Some analysts expect inflation could reach as high as 6% during the first half of the year from year-earlier levels.\par \par \uc2 The PBOC has two main anti-inflationary tools: interest rates and bank reserve requirements, or the share of deposits that lenders must hold with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of issuing as loans. But the PBOC adjusts interest rates less frequently and the changes tend to have less impact than in other big emerging markets. Some China experts say China relies too heavily on its ability to control the banking sector--almost all of which is state-owned, but which had been gaining more independence until the last few years.\par \par \uc2 "This is a big step backward in China's reform of its banking sector," said Steven Dunaway, a former China chief for the International Monetary Fund. "The problems associated with these distortions are beginning to catch up."\par \par \uc2 Since the start of last year, China has lifted reserve requirements seven times to a level of 19% for most banks to try to keep lending constrained. More hikes are widely expected in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Another big difference with other emerging markets: China tightly controls movements in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In other countries, when rates go up, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also often gains in value. That's part of the way monetary policy works-but not in China. If the yuan rose more freely, that would help reduce inflation.\par \par \uc2 Interest rates take on more of a symbolic role in China. "They are an important signal of the government's intentions but do not have a significant practical effect, especially when inflation-adjusted lending rates are so low," said Eswar Prasad, a China scholar at the Brookings Institution in Washington. That's because much lending is done to large state-owned enterprises, which aren't especially sensitive to changes in interest rates, he said, and small firms can't get much lending from banks anyway.\par \par \uc2 The 3% level that the PBOC set Tuesday for one-year deposits remains well below inflation rates, which does little to encourage Chinese households to put their money in banks rather than in more speculative investments like real estate. The 6% lending rate is slightly above the anticipated rate of inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese light touch on interest rates contrasts sharply with Brazil, another rapidly growing country worried about escalating inflation. When price rises there reached 6% in December, compared to the government's target of 4.5%, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the country's benchmark interest rate, already the highest among major economies worldwide, even higher. The rate, now at 11.25%, is expected to climb throughout this year.\par \par \uc2 India hasn't let its interest rates jump to Brazilian levels. Last month, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  upped its borrowing rate, or reverse repurchase rate, to 5.50%, and the lending rate, or repurchase rate, to 6.50%. But Brazil and India also let their exchange rates appreciate, which helps fight inflation by making imports less expensive.\par \par \uc2 China, however, sharply limits its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, despite exhortations from the U.S., Brazil, Europe and others that it let the exchange rate move higher. Chinese officials say they worry that too fast an appreciation could undermine the country's export sector. The yuan has gained only about 3.5% against the dollar since June.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner, on a visit to Brazil on Monday, urged Brazilian officials to help the U.S. press China to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate more rapidly. Both countries and other big exporters have argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan weak leads to artificially low prices for Chinese exports and unfair competition in global trade.\par \par \uc2 --Paulo Prada and Subhadip Sircar contributed to this article. [ 02-08-11 1223ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110208e728000ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
