\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Treasurys Tick Down On Hawkish Lacker\b0\par\par\uc2 1,384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2011\par 14:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Robert Flint, 212-416-2216; \par robert.flint@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1354 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys tick down on hawkish comments from Fed's Lacker. Even though he is not a voting member on rates this year and a well-known hawk, his comments that serious re-evaluation of QE2 is needed cause some jitterys for the embattled bond market. In recent trade, the benchmark 10-year note is 5/32 lower to yield 3.658%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1353 GMT [Dow Jones] Housing starts in Canada were roughly unchanged in Jan at an annualized rate of 170.4K units. Gains were centered in rural areas, while urban areas saw starts for both single units and multiples drop slightly, says CIBC World Markets. "We continue to expect a softening in housing starts in coming months as home prices moderate and the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  resumes its tightening cycle by mid-year," CIBC says. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1347 GMT [Dow Jones] With little US economic data on tap, investors will likely take USD trading cues from a roster of Fed officials, say analysts. Richmond's Lacker, Atlanta's Lockhart and Dallas' Fisher all speak Tuesday, but Fisher is the only FOMC voter this year. The big kahuna, Fed chairman Bernanke, testifies on Capitol Hill Wednesday. "The biggest near-term risk for the USD is a material shift in the rhetoric coming from the Fed," say Scotia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts. "We expect that the clouded picture on employment combined with historically low inflation rates will leave the Fed sounding dovish for some time to come." January's US employment report is still open to interpretation as to whether the glass is half-full or half-empty on a strong US recovery and pick-up in inflation this year, so the speakers' words will likely carry added weight, they say. EUR/USD recently traded at 1.3634 from 1.3583 late Mon, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1345 GMT [Dow Jones] The long bond is the outperformer as the selling in the embattled Treasury market has eased this week. Traders note the yield near 4.75% lure pension funds and insurance companies who buy to match their long-term obligations. The Fed is on deck for a bout of long-dated bond buying later this morning. The yield has bounced off Monday's peak of 4.771%, the highest level since April, and is recently trading flat at 4.702%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1332 GMT [Dow Jones] The International Council of Shopping Centers and Goldman Sachs Retail Chain Store Sales Index rose 2.2% in the week ended Saturday from the week before, ending four straight weeks of declines. "Weather continued to play havoc with retailers this past week," ICSC Chief Economist Michael Niemira said, while noting the turnaround. On a year-on-year basis, the reading rose 2.5%, also stronger than the previous week. For February, ICSC expects an overall comparable industry sales gain of between 2.5% to 3%.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (robert.flint@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1324 GMT (Dow Jones) The Swiss January unemployment report confirms the "positive labor market momentum remains largely unchanged," according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The data not only highlight the upbeat Swiss business sentiment, but can also be seen as a solid support for private consumption at the start of '11," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "As such, these are the data that have market participants looking for an eventual monetary policy tightening in Switzerland," he adds. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1320 GMT (Dow Jones) The move by Egypt's government to raise public sector wages by 15% is negative and is seen widening the country's budget deficit by 0.4-0.5 percentage points of GDP, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Notes, the biggest impact is likely to felt next year. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1310 GMT (Dow Jones) EUROPEAN SUMMARY: A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate hike and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Egyptian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support its beleaguered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were the highlights of an otherwise uninspiring session. USD weakness seen in Asia fed through into Europe although it was not one-way traffic. Mid-session China hiked all rates by 25bp, which caught the market on the hop coming one day before Chinese markets reopen from the Lunar holiday. The risk \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  immediately dipped on the move and while most quickly rebounded AUD continued to suffer due to the many trade links between the two countries. Meanwhile German December industrial production sank 1.5% against a 0.3% decline expected but again had minimal impact on the euro. Sterling had a rare underperforming session; early support from strong overnight RICS housing survey and BRC retail sales faded, with news the UK government has made the bank levy on profits permanent cited as one reason for the pound's slippage. GBP/USD currently at the day's low of 1.6084 from 1.6161, EUR/USD traded 1.3593-1.3667, USD/JPY a no-show between 81.99-82.21 while AUD/USD slipped to 1.0114 from 1.0192. Elsewhere, the Bank of Greece \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the rapid fall in the Egyptian pound seen since domestic banks reopened Monday. USD/EGP collapsed to 5.8751 after printing a fresh 10-year high of 5.9651 Tuesday. EUR/SEK printed yet another 10-year low of 8.7643. Away from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets core European government bonds trade flat although lower demand at the ECB's weekly and monthly cash tenders pushed short-term German yields higher. March bunds are currently -0.01 at 122.85, March gilts +0.02 at 115.85. The main European equity bourses trade 0.1% around par after taking a small dip on China's rate hike. Oil's $1.50 drop to a one-week low of $85.95 is all related to China while gold is a down 60c at $1350oz. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1305 GMT [Dow Jones] Traders shrugged off a second straight disappointing report on Germany's economy and an interest-rate hike by China to send the euro higher against the dollar. Some risk appetite returned to the market as market participants downplayed the large-than-expected drop in Germany industrial production in December. China hiked benchmark rates by 0.25 percentage points. The euro was at $1.3632 from $1.3582 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1304 GMT (Dow Jones) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  still expects another two additional rate hikes in China in the first-half of the year, based on the view that inflation will peak in 1Q 2011. "We expect consumer price inflation to again surge above 5% year-on-year in January from 4.4% year-on-year," the bank adds. Danske says Tuesday's rate hike suggests that China might hike more this year than it is currently expected. (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1249 GMT (Dow Jones) Egyptian bonds continue their recovery Tuesday as tensions continue to ease. Egypt's 3.75% 2020 bond is currently trading at 96.70, up from 92.19 at the height of the crisis. Meanwhile, Egypt's 6.875% bonds maturing April 30, 2040 are currently trading at 93.95 compared with a low Jan 31 of 88.50. The bond market recovery is broadly in line with the country's five-year CDS spread which is now trading at 343bps, around 90bps tighter than the late January high of 432bps. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1238 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate hike Tuesday should be supportive of assets in "Goldilocks Asia" countries such as Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, Thailand and Malaysia, as it removes uncertainty over Chinese policy, says Prasenjit K. Basu, chief Asia ex-Japan economist at Daiwa Capital Markets. "With export prospects bolstered by the surging U.S. economy, the PBOC has again demonstrated its determination to rein in CPI inflation and stabilize the property market. We believe China's CPI inflation is close to peaking, and this hike will make a salutary contribution toward ensuring that," he says. (natasha.brereton@dowjones.com) [ 02-08-11 0854ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110208e7280008q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
