erformer as the selling in the embattled Treasury market has eased this week. Traders note the yield near 4.75% lure pension funds and insurance companies who buy to match their long-term obligations. The Fed is on deck for a bout of long-dated bond buying later this morning. The yield has bounced off Monday's peak of 4.771%, the highest level since April, and is recently trading flat at 4.702%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1332 GMT [Dow Jones] The International Council of Shopping Centers and Goldman Sachs Retail Chain Store Sales Index rose 2.2% in the week ended Saturday from the week before, ending four straight weeks of declines. "Weather continued to play havoc with retailers this past week," ICSC Chief Economist Michael Niemira said, while noting the turnaround. On a year-on-year basis, the reading rose 2.5%, also stronger than the previous week. For February, ICSC expects an overall comparable industry sales gain of between 2.5% to 3%.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (robert.flint@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1324 GMT (Dow Jones) The Swiss January unemployment report confirms the "positive labor market momentum remains largely unchanged," according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The data not only highlight the upbeat Swiss business sentiment, but can also be seen as a solid support for private consumption at the start of '11," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "As such, these are the data that have market participants looking for an eventual monetary policy tightening in Switzerland," he adds. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1320 GMT (Dow Jones) The move by Egypt's government to raise public sector wages by 15% is negative and is seen widening the country's budget deficit by 0.4-0.5 percentage points of GDP, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Notes, the biggest impact is likely to felt next year. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1310 GMT (Dow Jones) EUROPEAN SUMMARY: A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate hike and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Egyptian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support its beleaguered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were the highlights of an otherwise uninspiring session. USD weakness seen in Asia fed through into Europe although it was not one-way traffic. Mid-session China hiked all rates by 25bp, which caught the market on the hop coming one day before Chinese markets reopen from the Lunar holiday. The risk \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  immediately dipped on the move and while most quickly rebounded AUD continued to suffer due to the many trade links between the two countries. Meanwhile German December industrial production sank 1.5% against a 0.3% decline expected but again had minimal impact on the euro. Sterling had a rare underperforming session; early support from strong overnight RICS housing survey and BRC retail sales faded, with news the UK government has made the bank levy on profits permanent cited as one reason for the pound's slippage. GBP/USD currently at the day's low of 1.6084 from 1.6161, EUR/USD traded 1.3593-1.3667, USD/JPY a no-show between 81.99-82.21 while AUD/USD slipped to 1.0114 from 1.0192. Elsewhere, the Bank of Greece \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the rapid fall in the Egyptian pound seen since domestic banks reopened Monday. USD/EGP collapsed to 5.8751 after printing a fresh 10-year high of 5.9651 Tuesday. EUR/SEK printed yet another 10-year low of 8.7643. Away from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets core European government bonds trade flat although lower demand at the ECB's weekly and monthly cash tenders pushed short-term German yields higher. March bunds are currently -0.01 at 122.85, March gilts +0.02 at 115.85. The main European equity bourses trade 0.1% around par after taking a small dip on China's rate hike. Oil's $1.50 drop to a one-week low of $85.95 is all related to China while gold is a down 60c at $1350oz. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1305 GMT [Dow Jones] Traders shrugged off a second straight disappointing report on Germany's economy and an interest-rate hike by China to send the euro higher against the dollar. Some risk appetite returned to the market as market participants downplayed the large-than-expected drop in Germany industrial production in December. China hiked benchmark rates by 0.25 percentage points. The euro was at $1.3632 from $1.3582 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1304 GMT (Dow Jones) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  still expects another two additional rate hikes in China in the first-half of the year, based on the view that inflation will peak in 1Q 2011. "We expect consumer price inflation to again surge above 5% year-on-year in January from 4.4% year-on-year," the bank adds. Danske says Tuesday's rate hike suggests that China might hike more this year than it is currently expected. (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1249 GMT (Dow Jones) Egyptian bonds continue their recovery Tuesday as tensions continue to ease. Egypt's 3.75% 2020 bond is currently trading at 96.70, up from 92.19 at the height of the crisis. Meanwhile, Egypt's 6.875% bonds maturing April 30, 2040 are currently trading at 93.95 compared with a low Jan 31 of 88.50. The bond market recovery is broadly in line with the country's five-year CDS spread which is now trading at 343bps, around 90bps tighter than the late January high of 432bps. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1238 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate hike Tuesday should be supportive of assets in "Goldilocks Asia" countries such as Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, Thailand and Malaysia, as it removes uncertainty over Chinese policy, says Prasenjit K. Basu, chief Asia ex-Japan economist at Daiwa Capital Markets. "With export prospects bolstered by the surging U.S. economy, the PBOC has again demonstrated its determination to rein in CPI inflation and stabilize the property market. We believe China's CPI inflation is close to peaking, and this hike will make a salutary contribution toward ensuring that," he says. (natasha.brereton@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1230 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spreads are 12 bps higher to 785 bps as the market shrugs off a small but positive T-Bill auction which was largely expected and factored-in. Turnover on the local HDAT and European trading platform adds only to EUR15M. "News that a small Danish bank is insolvent and will be nationalized and unwound spooked the GGB market because the senior bond holders will take losses," says a bond analyst. "All the euro zone periphery is moving wider and the market mood suggests that the trend will continue as profit taking sets in and risk appetite eases." (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par +44-20-7842-9464; markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-08-11 0853ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110208e7280008p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
