ernational Council of Shopping Centers and Goldman Sachs Retail Chain Store Sales Index rose 2.2% in the week ended Saturday from the week before, ending four straight weeks of declines. "Weather continued to play havoc with retailers this past week," ICSC Chief Economist Michael Niemira said, while noting the turnaround. On a year-on-year basis, the reading rose 2.5%, also stronger than the previous week. For February, ICSC expects an overall comparable industry sales gain of between 2.5% to 3%.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (robert.flint@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1324 GMT (Dow Jones) The Swiss January unemployment report confirms the "positive labor market momentum remains largely unchanged," according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The data not only highlight the upbeat Swiss business sentiment, but can also be seen as a solid support for private consumption at the start of '11," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "As such, these are the data that have market participants looking for an eventual monetary policy tightening in Switzerland," he adds. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1320 GMT (Dow Jones) The move by Egypt's government to raise public sector wages by 15% is negative and is seen widening the country's budget deficit by 0.4-0.5 percentage points of GDP, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Notes, the biggest impact is likely to felt next year. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1310 GMT (Dow Jones) EUROPEAN SUMMARY: A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate hike and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Egyptian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support its beleaguered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were the highlights of an otherwise uninspiring session. USD weakness seen in Asia fed through into Europe although it was not