Some risk appetite returned to the market as market participants downplayed the large-than-expected drop in Germany industrial production in December. China hiked benchmark rates by 0.25 percentage points. The euro was at $1.3632 from $1.3582 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stephen.bernard@dowjones.com) \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1304 GMT (Dow Jones) \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danske Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  still expects another two additional rate hikes in China in the first-half of the year, based on the view that inflation will peak in 1Q 2011. "We expect consumer price inflation to again surge above 5% year-on-year in January from 4.4% year-on-year," the bank adds. Danske says Tuesday's rate hike suggests that China might hike more this year than it is currently expected. (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1249 GMT (Dow Jones) Egyptian bonds continue their recovery Tuesday as tensions continue to ease. Egypt's 3.75% 2020 bond is currently trading at 96.70, up from 92.19 at the height of the crisis. Meanwhile, Egypt's 6.875% bonds maturing April 30, 2040 are currently trading at 93.95 compared with a low Jan 31 of 88.50. The bond market recovery is broadly in line with the country's five-year CDS spread which is now trading at 343bps, around 90bps tighter than the late January high of 432bps. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1238 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate hike Tuesday should be supportive of assets in "Goldilocks Asia" countries such as Korea, Taiwan, Singapore, Thailand and Malaysia, as it removes uncertainty over Chinese policy, says Prasenjit K. Basu, chief Asia ex-Japan economist at Daiwa Capital Markets. "With export prospects bolstered by the surging U.S. economy, the PBOC has again demonstrated its determination to rein in CPI inflation and stabilize the property market. We believe China's CPI inflation is close to peaking, and this hike will make a salutary contribution toward ensuring that," he says. (natasha.brereton@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1230 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spreads are 12 bps higher to 785 bps as the market shrugs off a small but positive T-Bill auction which was largely expected and factored-in. Turnover on the local HDAT and European trading platform adds only to EUR15M. "News that a small Danish bank is insolvent and will be nationalized and unwound spooked the GGB market because the senior bond holders will take losses," says a bond analyst. "All the euro zone periphery is moving wider and the market mood suggests that the trend will continue as profit taking sets in and risk appetite eases." (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par +44-20-7842-9464; markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1228 GMT (Dow Jones)--Troika Dialog recommends going long the ruble against the dollar euro basket to benefit from positive Russia sentiment and higher oil prices. "If oil prices stay at this level, or at least do not fall below $80/bbl, we should see a much stronger ruble. The tax payment period, which starts next week, will provide further support to the ruble. Our next target is RUB33.50 versus the basket." Ruble now trading at RUB34.09 against the basket, weaker by 0.3%. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com) [ 02-08-11 0847ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110208e7280008n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
