\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(2/8) US, Brazil To Press China On Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 495 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2011\par 06:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WSJ(2/8) US, Brazil To Press China On Yuan\par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) By Paulo Prada\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil -- Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner, on a visit Monday to Latin America's biggest economy, urged Brazilian officials to help the U.S. pressure China to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate.\par \par \uc2 Though officials in public shied away from specifics of any plan to coordinate calls for a stronger yuan, a person familiar with the discussions said Brazil and the U.S. may speak with a common voice on the issue in a coming meeting of the Group of 20 major economies.\par \par \uc2 Both countries and other big exporters have argued that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yuan weak leads to artificially low prices for Chinese exports and unfair competition in global trade.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Geithner's one-day visit, laying the groundwork for a planned trip to Brazil by President Barack Obama next month, reflects growing recognition that Brazil's increasing economic heft and good relations with other developing markets could be a useful tool in aligning global economic interests. In addition to a meeting with President Dilma Rousseff, the Treasury secretary met with Brazil's finance minister and the president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Brazil and the United States will increasingly work together to build a more stable, stronger multilateral system," Mr. Geithner said following a meeting with the president. "We have a very strong economic relationship with common interests."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's willingness to join the U.S. in its calls for a stronger yuan follows a shift by Ms. Rousseff to be more vocal in relations with Beijing than her predecessor, former President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva. Though booming commodities exports to China are behind much of Brazil's continuing boom, a flood of Chinese imports is increasingly crippling Brazilian manufacturers' ability to compete. Shortly after the new administration took office, Trade Minister Fernando Pimentel said Ms. Rousseff, on a trip to Beijing in April, plans to press the issue with Chinese leaders.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian officials say any efforts to join the U.S. on the issue don't diminish the country's unease with the U.S.'s monetary policy. Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buoyed by massive flows of speculative capital, has soared more than 35% against the dollar in the past two years. Because that, too, hurts the country's exporters, Brazilian officials have called for the U.S. to take steps to fortify a dollar that remains weak by historical standards.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Geithner lauded Brazil for being "pragmatic" in efforts to curb the appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real. But such steps, which have included higher taxes for short-term investments with foreign capital and higher fees for banks that make big bets on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , can later lead to imbalances, he warned. "You cannot make economic policy in isolation," Mr. Geithner said.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110209e7280063h\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 T